50
00:05:41,670 --> 00:05:43,620
which is always
dangerous, but I wanted

51
00:05:43,620 --> 00:05:46,340
to include a couple
additional ones.

52
00:05:46,340 --> 00:05:48,780
OK, let's see if we can make
this do the right thing.

53
00:05:48,780 --> 00:05:49,590
OK.

54
00:05:49,590 --> 00:05:51,870
So the second one
should be true.

55
00:05:51,870 --> 00:05:54,340
This is basically
the UCB algorithm,

56
00:05:54,340 --> 00:05:56,550
which is this is the
empirical estimate

57
00:05:56,550 --> 00:05:58,030
of the performance of each arm.

58
00:05:58,030 --> 00:06:01,020
So in the case where you just
have a finite set of arms, which

59
00:06:01,020 --> 00:06:03,810
we can also think of as
a finite set of actions,

60
00:06:03,810 --> 00:06:06,990
we just look at what
their average reward was.

61
00:06:06,990 --> 00:06:12,240
Nt of a was how many times have
we pulled action a after t time

62
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:12,990
steps.

63
00:06:12,990 --> 00:06:16,020
And log of f of delta
was just the term

64
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:19,170
that we had to try to express
the dependence on delta.

65
00:06:19,170 --> 00:06:22,500
Delta was used to look
at confidence intervals

66
00:06:22,500 --> 00:06:25,200
that we were using for the
upper confidence bound.

67
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:27,720
So this is true.

68
00:06:27,720 --> 00:06:31,050
The third one is also true.

69
00:06:31,050 --> 00:06:34,950
So, in general, with our
confidence intervals,

70
00:06:34,950 --> 00:06:38,420
you will be selecting all arms
an infinite number of times.

71
00:06:38,420 --> 00:06:42,190
But it might be
really slow later on.

72
00:06:42,190 --> 00:06:44,440
Let's say you have a really
big gap between arms.

73
00:06:44,440 --> 00:06:48,460
Then that log term--

74
00:06:48,460 --> 00:06:50,830
and you'll have a t dependence
in there, in general.

75
00:06:50,830 --> 00:06:52,580
That will continue
to grow a little bit.

76
00:06:52,580 --> 00:06:55,600
So you'll sample another arm
again, which helps with the fact

77
00:06:55,600 --> 00:06:57,280
that you might have
been really unlucky

78
00:06:57,280 --> 00:06:59,950
and gotten a really weird
estimate of the arm performance,

79
00:06:59,950 --> 00:07:01,860
so far.

80
00:07:01,860 --> 00:07:02,360
OK.

81
00:07:02,360 --> 00:07:04,210
This one was a
little bit subtle.

82
00:07:04,210 --> 00:07:06,585
And I realize it could
be not quite clear here

83
00:07:06,585 --> 00:07:07,960
whether I was
asking you to think

84
00:07:07,960 --> 00:07:12,820
about the T over delta part or
the 1 over square root Nt of a.

85
00:07:12,820 --> 00:07:15,520
I wanted you to focus
on the first thing.

86
00:07:15,520 --> 00:07:19,840
So what this is saying
here is that, instead

87
00:07:19,840 --> 00:07:23,530
of shrinking our confidence
intervals by a rate of 1

88
00:07:23,530 --> 00:07:27,850
over square root Nt, we're
shrinking them at a rate of n

89
00:07:27,850 --> 00:07:29,870
to the minus fourth.

90
00:07:29,870 --> 00:07:30,950
Let me just write that.

91
00:07:35,910 --> 00:07:39,382
[ERASING WHITEBOARD]

92
00:07:40,960 --> 00:07:42,380
That's pretty squeaky.

93
00:07:42,380 --> 00:07:43,410
OK.

94
00:07:43,410 --> 00:07:43,910
All right.

95
00:07:43,910 --> 00:07:46,320
So let me just give--
so we're shrinking it.

96
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:53,810
So will that mean that
our confidence intervals

97
00:07:53,810 --> 00:07:58,490
are wider or narrower for
the same number of counts?

98
00:07:58,490 --> 00:08:06,170
So let's say versus Nt of
a to the minus one half.

99
00:08:06,170 --> 00:08:09,480
So, for example, if Nt
of a is equal to 100,

100
00:08:09,480 --> 00:08:11,900
you've pulled this
arm 100 times.

101
00:08:11,900 --> 00:08:16,545
Which of these two is going to
be bigger, the one on the left

102
00:08:16,545 --> 00:08:17,545
or the one on the right?

103
00:08:21,315 --> 00:08:22,190
The one on the right.

104
00:08:22,190 --> 00:08:22,810
That's right.

105
00:08:22,810 --> 00:08:24,785
So instead of it being--

106
00:08:24,785 --> 00:08:26,130
oh, the other way around.

107
00:08:26,130 --> 00:08:29,990
So this is going to be-- so if
you have 100 to the minus 1/4

108
00:08:29,990 --> 00:08:33,919
versus 100 to the minus 1/2,
this is going to be 1/10.

109
00:08:33,919 --> 00:08:37,760
And this is going to be
approximately 1 over 3.

110
00:08:37,760 --> 00:08:39,480
There's several
different inverses here.

111
00:08:39,480 --> 00:08:40,940
I know.

112
00:08:40,940 --> 00:08:43,260
What this means, basically,
is that we're growing--

113
00:08:43,260 --> 00:08:46,940
we're shrinking our
confidence intervals slower.

114
00:08:46,940 --> 00:08:51,330
So another thing you might
see often, as a bonus term--

115
00:08:51,330 --> 00:08:53,910
DeepMind often uses
this, in particular,

116
00:08:53,910 --> 00:08:57,670
for some of their algorithms,
is "to the minus 1."

117
00:08:57,670 --> 00:08:59,110
So that's a faster rate.

118
00:08:59,110 --> 00:09:04,470
So then that one
would be 1 over 100.

119
00:09:04,470 --> 00:09:05,970
You can think of
these as trading

120
00:09:05,970 --> 00:09:07,800
off different amounts
of, essentially,

121
00:09:07,800 --> 00:09:09,930
how much exploration
you're going to get.

122
00:09:09,930 --> 00:09:13,020
Because this is
saying how quickly

123
00:09:13,020 --> 00:09:14,520
are you collapsing
your confidence

124
00:09:14,520 --> 00:09:16,920
interval as you have more data.

125
00:09:16,920 --> 00:09:18,960
Now, as somebody
pointed-- was asking me

126
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:21,210
about-- when I was going
around, they were like, well,

127
00:09:21,210 --> 00:09:23,290
we didn't just
randomly pick this.

128
00:09:23,290 --> 00:09:25,320
We picked this because
of the uncertainty

129
00:09:25,320 --> 00:09:28,120
bounds that we derived
from Hoeffding.

130
00:09:28,120 --> 00:09:30,990
So Hoeffding said, if you
have an empirical estimate

131
00:09:30,990 --> 00:09:34,480
of your mean, how far away could
that be from the true mean?

132
00:09:34,480 --> 00:09:36,990
Well, under pretty
mild conditions

133
00:09:36,990 --> 00:09:38,860
about your variable
being bounded,

134
00:09:38,860 --> 00:09:43,170
we could get this 1
over squared N rate.

135
00:09:43,170 --> 00:09:46,090
So someone was asking me,
very reasonably, well,

136
00:09:46,090 --> 00:09:48,840
why would you pick, say,
this or something else?

137
00:09:48,840 --> 00:09:51,120
You might pick this
because you just

138
00:09:51,120 --> 00:09:52,770
don't want to explore as much.

139
00:09:52,770 --> 00:09:55,990
So even though this
holds for our theory,

140
00:09:55,990 --> 00:09:58,980
it's often somewhat
conservative in practice.

141
00:09:58,980 --> 00:10:02,520
So you might just pick something
like a faster exploration rate

142
00:10:02,520 --> 00:10:07,258
because, empirically, you
want to just explore less.

143
00:10:07,258 --> 00:10:09,300
And you could think of
that as being related back

144
00:10:09,300 --> 00:10:11,760
to what we saw with
PPO, that a lot

145
00:10:11,760 --> 00:10:13,450
of their theoretical
derivation said,

146
00:10:13,450 --> 00:10:15,400
this is what your
step size should be,

147
00:10:15,400 --> 00:10:18,965
but it was way too conservative
for most realistic applications.

148
00:10:18,965 --> 00:10:20,340
So they just
changed it, and they

149
00:10:20,340 --> 00:10:21,960
introduced the clipping thing.

150
00:10:21,960 --> 00:10:24,360
On the other hand,
there might be

151
00:10:24,360 --> 00:10:27,150
cases for which you
might not be sure you

152
00:10:27,150 --> 00:10:29,847
could get this sort of rate.

153
00:10:29,847 --> 00:10:31,680
Or you might have other
reasons to think you

154
00:10:31,680 --> 00:10:33,250
might need more exploration.

155
00:10:33,250 --> 00:10:36,030
So, for example, maybe
things are non-stationary.

156
00:10:36,030 --> 00:10:38,250
And you think, my
customer preferences

157
00:10:38,250 --> 00:10:39,820
are actually changing over time.

158
00:10:39,820 --> 00:10:42,720
And so I want to
explore more, over time,

159
00:10:42,720 --> 00:10:46,570
than I would if I assumed
that I was stationary.

160
00:10:46,570 --> 00:10:50,430
And we'll talk more about
stationarity in just a second.

161
00:10:50,430 --> 00:10:54,000
So given all of that, this
means that this expression

162
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,310
would actually have wider
confidence intervals

163
00:10:56,310 --> 00:10:58,200
and probably a higher
upper confidence bound

164
00:10:58,200 --> 00:10:59,970
than our original
algorithm, which

165
00:10:59,970 --> 00:11:03,730
means that we would still expect
that, over time, it will learn--

166
00:11:03,730 --> 00:11:04,230
[SNEEZES]

167
00:11:04,230 --> 00:11:07,450
--pull the optimal arm-- bless
you-- more than any other arms.

168
00:11:07,450 --> 00:11:10,740
But it probably won't have
as tight regret bounds

169
00:11:10,740 --> 00:11:14,610
because we may be
exploring too much.

170
00:11:14,610 --> 00:11:17,490
Now, this was an
interesting one.

171
00:11:17,490 --> 00:11:21,480
Will this, if we add this
particular bonus term,

172
00:11:21,480 --> 00:11:24,090
make the algorithm
optimistic, with respect

173
00:11:24,090 --> 00:11:25,310
to the empirical rewards?

174
00:11:50,130 --> 00:11:51,570
Somebody want to
say if it's going

175
00:11:51,570 --> 00:11:53,440
to make-- if we add
on a bonus term,

176
00:11:53,440 --> 00:11:55,168
will it make it
optimistic, with respect

177
00:11:55,168 --> 00:11:56,210
to the empirical rewards?

178
00:11:59,510 --> 00:12:02,515
Just the empirical rewards, so
compared to your empirical mean.

179
00:12:06,813 --> 00:12:08,605
I'm not trying to make
it a trick question.

180
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:11,940
Yes.

181
00:12:11,940 --> 00:12:13,170
Yes, exactly.

182
00:12:13,170 --> 00:12:16,172
So if you just add 20 to
your empirical estimate,

183
00:12:16,172 --> 00:12:17,630
it will be optimistic,
with respect

184
00:12:17,630 --> 00:12:19,020
to your empirical estimate.

185
00:12:19,020 --> 00:12:21,410
But is it guaranteed
to be optimistic,

186
00:12:21,410 --> 00:12:22,765
with respect to your true mean?

187
00:12:32,880 --> 00:12:37,380
So imagine if I had
said B was 0.001.

188
00:12:37,380 --> 00:12:39,390
It would still make
it be optimistic,

189
00:12:39,390 --> 00:12:41,335
with respect to your
empirical rewards.

190
00:12:41,335 --> 00:12:42,960
But would it necessarily
be optimistic,

191
00:12:42,960 --> 00:12:44,252
with respect to your true mean?

192
00:12:48,770 --> 00:12:49,710
In general, no, right?

193
00:12:49,710 --> 00:12:53,380
So if you think back
to our bandits, which

194
00:12:53,380 --> 00:12:55,850
just had binary
rewards, let's say

195
00:12:55,850 --> 00:12:59,720
you have a coin that actually
has a 0.5 probability of getting

196
00:12:59,720 --> 00:13:01,370
a heads, which we'll call a 1.

197
00:13:01,370 --> 00:13:03,330
If you flip it once
and you get a tails,

198
00:13:03,330 --> 00:13:06,000
your empirical
estimate will be 0.

199
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:11,340
If you add a bonus of 0.01, your
empirical estimate will be 0.01.

200
00:13:11,340 --> 00:13:14,300
The true value of the
mean is still 0.5.

201
00:13:14,300 --> 00:13:16,850
So one of the key ideas
from using Hoeffding

202
00:13:16,850 --> 00:13:18,650
and explicit upper
confidence bounds

203
00:13:18,650 --> 00:13:21,530
is that, in general, it's
not easy to figure out

204
00:13:21,530 --> 00:13:24,080
a simple bonus term
you can add in order

205
00:13:24,080 --> 00:13:26,300
to make things optimistic.

206
00:13:26,300 --> 00:13:29,540
And so that's why you
might, in general, want

207
00:13:29,540 --> 00:13:33,200
to be using these Hoeffding
or other explicitly derived

208
00:13:33,200 --> 00:13:36,800
confidence intervals.

209
00:13:36,800 --> 00:13:37,420
OK, great.

210
00:13:37,420 --> 00:13:38,670
And then the last one is true.

211
00:13:38,670 --> 00:13:40,503
Does anybody have any
questions about these?

212
00:13:40,503 --> 00:13:41,540
Yeah?

213
00:13:41,540 --> 00:13:45,650
Can you explain
number three again?

214
00:13:45,650 --> 00:13:47,320
I'm not sure whether
dependence on--

215
00:13:50,040 --> 00:13:53,070
It's inside of here.

216
00:13:53,070 --> 00:13:55,768
So if you go back to the
slides from last time--

217
00:13:55,768 --> 00:13:57,185
let me see if I
just have them up.

218
00:14:00,760 --> 00:14:03,263
Yeah, we create these
upper confidence bounds.

219
00:14:10,260 --> 00:14:14,110
So, in general, we define
these upper confidence bounds.

220
00:14:14,110 --> 00:14:16,170
We talked about how
we need the bounds

221
00:14:16,170 --> 00:14:18,630
to hold over all-time steps, t.

222
00:14:18,630 --> 00:14:22,667
And so, in fact, this was
not a perfect expression

223
00:14:22,667 --> 00:14:24,750
that we're going to have
some sort of t dependence

224
00:14:24,750 --> 00:14:26,820
inside of the log.

225
00:14:26,820 --> 00:14:29,970
And in general, we'll have
something like t or t squared

226
00:14:29,970 --> 00:14:31,060
inside of the log.

227
00:14:31,060 --> 00:14:34,680
And so that will introduce
a dependence on the time--

228
00:14:34,680 --> 00:14:37,020
either the time step, so far,
or your total time horizon

229
00:14:37,020 --> 00:14:38,603
inside of your upper
confidence bound.

230
00:14:41,770 --> 00:14:43,270
Any other questions
about this part?

231
00:14:48,410 --> 00:14:50,420
OK.

232
00:14:50,420 --> 00:14:51,680
All right.

233
00:14:51,680 --> 00:14:54,110
I'll make sure that the
solutions are aligned.

234
00:14:54,110 --> 00:14:56,970
So last time we talked
about Bayesians.

235
00:14:56,970 --> 00:14:57,470
Sorry.

236
00:14:57,470 --> 00:15:01,100
We talked about bandits, which
were this single-state version

237
00:15:01,100 --> 00:15:03,140
of Markov decision processes.

238
00:15:03,140 --> 00:15:05,720
Your actions didn't make any
difference to the next state

239
00:15:05,720 --> 00:15:07,760
because you're always
in a single state.

240
00:15:07,760 --> 00:15:10,100
We talked about how people
often use the word "arms"

241
00:15:10,100 --> 00:15:12,170
as an equivalent for actions.

242
00:15:12,170 --> 00:15:13,880
And, there, we were
trying to be really

243
00:15:13,880 --> 00:15:17,000
explicit about uncertainty
over the rewards.

244
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:20,330
And we talked about algorithm,
upper confidence bounds

245
00:15:20,330 --> 00:15:22,190
for trying to be
optimistic, with respect

246
00:15:22,190 --> 00:15:23,412
to that uncertainty.

247
00:15:23,412 --> 00:15:25,370
Well, today what we're
going to focus on mostly

248
00:15:25,370 --> 00:15:26,510
is Bayesian bandits.

249
00:15:26,510 --> 00:15:29,090
And we'll get there
in a few minutes.

250
00:15:29,090 --> 00:15:31,577
Before we do that, I think
it's nice to think about--

251
00:15:31,577 --> 00:15:34,160
I think it's exciting to think
about all the application areas

252
00:15:34,160 --> 00:15:35,330
where these come up.

253
00:15:35,330 --> 00:15:37,820
And I wanted to go through
this example, which

254
00:15:37,820 --> 00:15:40,550
I think I mentioned
briefly in lecture one,

255
00:15:40,550 --> 00:15:43,670
just again, to think about all
the complexities which come up

256
00:15:43,670 --> 00:15:45,870
when we want to try to
use these in practice

257
00:15:45,870 --> 00:15:49,080
and where bandit algorithms,
in particular, might be used.

258
00:15:49,080 --> 00:15:50,760
So this is a really
beautiful paper

259
00:15:50,760 --> 00:15:55,060
by Hamsa Bastani, which was
in Nature a few years ago.

260
00:15:55,060 --> 00:15:57,750
And they were trying to tackle a
really important problem, which

261
00:15:57,750 --> 00:16:00,750
was, at the time, as
everything was shutting down

262
00:16:00,750 --> 00:16:03,060
with COVID, all
these countries had

263
00:16:03,060 --> 00:16:07,230
to decide on a quarantine
protocol and who to test.

264
00:16:07,230 --> 00:16:10,920
So, before, a number of
countries basically almost

265
00:16:10,920 --> 00:16:12,670
entirely shut down travel.

266
00:16:12,670 --> 00:16:16,410
But particularly in the
beginning, people were letting--

267
00:16:16,410 --> 00:16:18,730
And even then, often,
there might be exceptions.

268
00:16:18,730 --> 00:16:23,400
So as people come into
a border crossing,

269
00:16:23,400 --> 00:16:25,590
countries had to
decide who to test.

270
00:16:25,590 --> 00:16:27,810
Now, they couldn't
necessarily test everybody

271
00:16:27,810 --> 00:16:30,400
because resources are finite.

272
00:16:30,400 --> 00:16:32,518
They're also having
testing facilities

273
00:16:32,518 --> 00:16:34,810
they're using for testing
all of their own individuals.

274
00:16:34,810 --> 00:16:36,130
And tests are expensive.

275
00:16:36,130 --> 00:16:38,230
In addition, when
someone is tested,

276
00:16:38,230 --> 00:16:40,392
they are going to ask
them to quarantine.

277
00:16:40,392 --> 00:16:42,100
Depending on where
you were in the world,

278
00:16:42,100 --> 00:16:44,620
that might actually have been
funded by the government.

279
00:16:44,620 --> 00:16:47,220
So you have to go to a
quarantine hotel, which also

280
00:16:47,220 --> 00:16:48,387
costs the government money.

281
00:16:48,387 --> 00:16:50,220
So there are a lot of
reasons, in this case,

282
00:16:50,220 --> 00:16:52,595
that your resources are limited
and you don't necessarily

283
00:16:52,595 --> 00:16:53,710
want to test everyone.

284
00:16:53,710 --> 00:16:56,430
Also, in general, it may not
be necessary to test everybody,

285
00:16:56,430 --> 00:17:00,450
if you're trying to minimize
the probability of letting

286
00:17:00,450 --> 00:17:03,688
in people that have COVID,
in terms of limiting spread.

287
00:17:03,688 --> 00:17:05,730
So if there's someone
that's from somewhere where

288
00:17:05,730 --> 00:17:08,355
there's no COVID, then you don't
necessarily need to test them.

289
00:17:08,355 --> 00:17:09,760
So this is the setting.

290
00:17:09,760 --> 00:17:13,150
What happens is that when
people were coming into Greece,

291
00:17:13,150 --> 00:17:15,849
they would submit
a form in advance,

292
00:17:15,849 --> 00:17:19,619
like when you go to the airport
or before you go, et cetera.

293
00:17:19,619 --> 00:17:23,130
And then what they would do is
they had this approach called

294
00:17:23,130 --> 00:17:26,190
Eva, which tried to
use the prior testing

295
00:17:26,190 --> 00:17:30,240
results to figure out who to
actually test when they came.

296
00:17:30,240 --> 00:17:32,910
So what would happen is that,
then, when somebody comes,

297
00:17:32,910 --> 00:17:34,740
like the next day,
either they would say,

298
00:17:34,740 --> 00:17:36,240
we're not going to
test you at all--

299
00:17:36,240 --> 00:17:38,070
and then you would
leave the premises.

300
00:17:38,070 --> 00:17:40,982
Or, for a subset of
people, based on the form,

301
00:17:40,982 --> 00:17:42,690
based on where they
were coming and based

302
00:17:42,690 --> 00:17:46,770
on prior results they had, they
would decide to test someone.

303
00:17:46,770 --> 00:17:49,770
Then, after you got that test,
you would send it to a lab.

304
00:17:49,770 --> 00:17:53,240
And it normally would
take 24 to 48 hours.

305
00:17:53,240 --> 00:17:54,990
I don't remember exactly
what kind of test

306
00:17:54,990 --> 00:17:55,870
they were using there.

307
00:17:55,870 --> 00:17:57,370
Maybe it was some
sort of rapid PCR.

308
00:17:57,370 --> 00:17:59,670
I don't remember.

309
00:17:59,670 --> 00:18:01,930
Those would go to
a central database.

310
00:18:01,930 --> 00:18:04,380
And then they would
use those results.

311
00:18:04,380 --> 00:18:07,440
All these people would
quarantine for 24 to 96 hours

312
00:18:07,440 --> 00:18:08,860
or so, during this time period.

313
00:18:08,860 --> 00:18:10,193
They would get the results back.

314
00:18:10,193 --> 00:18:13,183
If you're clear, you
can go and proceed.

315
00:18:13,183 --> 00:18:15,100
Otherwise, you need to
continue to quarantine.

316
00:18:15,100 --> 00:18:17,017
And then they're going
to use this information

317
00:18:17,017 --> 00:18:20,550
to go back to Eva and
update their algorithm.

318
00:18:20,550 --> 00:18:24,690
So this is really cool
because this is an opportunity

319
00:18:24,690 --> 00:18:28,270
to try to be very
careful about resources,

320
00:18:28,270 --> 00:18:31,035
but really do so in a
way that still preserves

321
00:18:31,035 --> 00:18:32,910
the safety of the
individuals in the country,

322
00:18:32,910 --> 00:18:35,130
as much as possible,
and the public health.

323
00:18:35,130 --> 00:18:37,570
So I like that, in
the Bastani paper,

324
00:18:37,570 --> 00:18:40,260
they describe this as a
non-stationary contextual batch

325
00:18:40,260 --> 00:18:43,610
bandit problem with delayed
feedback and constraints.

326
00:18:43,610 --> 00:18:44,110
OK.

327
00:18:44,110 --> 00:18:45,780
So that's quite a mouthful.

328
00:18:45,780 --> 00:18:47,970
But I think it's really
nice to think about--

329
00:18:47,970 --> 00:18:50,280
as we go from this simple
setting of just thinking

330
00:18:50,280 --> 00:18:53,640
there are k arms, we can think
about all the practical things

331
00:18:53,640 --> 00:18:55,890
that we might have to
deal with in this setting.

332
00:18:55,890 --> 00:18:58,960
So, here, in some ways,
the k is very small.

333
00:18:58,960 --> 00:18:59,850
It's only two.

334
00:18:59,850 --> 00:19:00,960
Either you're going
to test someone,

335
00:19:00,960 --> 00:19:02,410
or you're not
going to test them.

336
00:19:02,410 --> 00:19:05,850
So it's a very small action
space, which is nice.

337
00:19:05,850 --> 00:19:09,040
In this case, compared to
what we've seen, so far--

338
00:19:09,040 --> 00:19:11,350
but we'll see this case later.

339
00:19:11,350 --> 00:19:12,850
We're going to have context.

340
00:19:12,850 --> 00:19:15,370
Context, you can think of
as just being like states.

341
00:19:15,370 --> 00:19:17,610
So people will have
a feature vector that

342
00:19:17,610 --> 00:19:19,830
describes what country
they're coming from,

343
00:19:19,830 --> 00:19:22,710
a bunch of other details
about them, and that gives you

344
00:19:22,710 --> 00:19:24,780
a state that we're
going to use to decide

345
00:19:24,780 --> 00:19:26,770
whether or not to test someone.

346
00:19:26,770 --> 00:19:28,800
So that's why it's contextual.

347
00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:33,690
It's non-stationary because
COVID was constantly evolving

348
00:19:33,690 --> 00:19:36,330
and, often, a lot of the
information we were getting

349
00:19:36,330 --> 00:19:37,235
was lagged.

350
00:19:37,235 --> 00:19:38,610
So if you're in
Greece, you might

351
00:19:38,610 --> 00:19:41,010
be able to see information
from Sweden and from China

352
00:19:41,010 --> 00:19:42,600
and from the US.

353
00:19:42,600 --> 00:19:45,220
But all of that information
is often, likely,

354
00:19:45,220 --> 00:19:47,050
probably at a population level.

355
00:19:47,050 --> 00:19:49,110
Those people may or may
not be the same people

356
00:19:49,110 --> 00:19:50,520
that are traveling to Greece.

357
00:19:50,520 --> 00:19:53,280
Probably, in general,
they're different.

358
00:19:53,280 --> 00:19:57,125
And because of the lag, it
may or may not be informative.

359
00:19:57,125 --> 00:19:58,500
And in fact, in
their paper, they

360
00:19:58,500 --> 00:20:00,125
argue a lot of that
information was not

361
00:20:00,125 --> 00:20:03,600
as informative as this kind
of real-time information.

362
00:20:03,600 --> 00:20:04,990
It's batched.

363
00:20:04,990 --> 00:20:07,290
What I mean by that is that--

364
00:20:07,290 --> 00:20:08,980
and we'll see this more today.

365
00:20:08,980 --> 00:20:13,300
You don't get to make a decision
after every test or not test.

366
00:20:13,300 --> 00:20:15,120
You don't see the
result immediately.

367
00:20:15,120 --> 00:20:17,610
So what happens
here is they say,

368
00:20:17,610 --> 00:20:19,478
200 people fly in on a plane.

369
00:20:19,478 --> 00:20:21,520
You have to decide, for
every single one of them,

370
00:20:21,520 --> 00:20:23,620
whether or not you're
going to test them.

371
00:20:23,620 --> 00:20:26,505
And then you wait two days.

372
00:20:26,505 --> 00:20:28,590
[LAUGHS] So it's this
delayed feedback,

373
00:20:28,590 --> 00:20:31,140
and you have to make a decision
for everybody before you

374
00:20:31,140 --> 00:20:33,030
get to observe that feedback.

375
00:20:33,030 --> 00:20:35,470
And so that makes
it quite tricky.

376
00:20:35,470 --> 00:20:37,080
And we'll talk
more about why that

377
00:20:37,080 --> 00:20:39,330
might be tricky for some of
the upper confidence bound

378
00:20:39,330 --> 00:20:41,610
algorithms we've seen so far.

379
00:20:41,610 --> 00:20:44,220
I think this batching
is really important for

380
00:20:44,220 --> 00:20:46,000
many, many application areas.

381
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:47,940
So if you think back
to our guest lecture

382
00:20:47,940 --> 00:20:51,040
and you think about direct
preference optimization,

383
00:20:51,040 --> 00:20:53,460
this is another area
where, in general, you're

384
00:20:53,460 --> 00:20:56,550
going to be able to get a
batch of data, label it all

385
00:20:56,550 --> 00:20:58,170
and then continue.

386
00:20:58,170 --> 00:21:00,150
So in some of the work
that my lab is doing

387
00:21:00,150 --> 00:21:01,680
and some other
people's work, when

388
00:21:01,680 --> 00:21:03,630
we're thinking about
doing adaptive data

389
00:21:03,630 --> 00:21:05,800
collection for
preference optimization,

390
00:21:05,800 --> 00:21:08,430
we, again, need it to be
able to handle this much more

391
00:21:08,430 --> 00:21:11,880
realistic batch setting,
compared to getting information

392
00:21:11,880 --> 00:21:13,430
after each decision.

393
00:21:16,860 --> 00:21:19,230
So the delayed feedback
is this 24 to 48 hours.

394
00:21:19,230 --> 00:21:21,600
And the final thing
is constraints.

395
00:21:21,600 --> 00:21:25,380
So there are lots of constraints
in this setting, which

396
00:21:25,380 --> 00:21:27,840
also generally changed the
setting from a lot of the ones

397
00:21:27,840 --> 00:21:29,190
we've thought about, so far.

398
00:21:29,190 --> 00:21:31,770
So one is that you might
have resource constraints.

399
00:21:31,770 --> 00:21:34,860
You might say, at most, we
can handle, let's say, 100.

400
00:21:34,860 --> 00:21:36,610
I forget exactly what
it was in the paper.

401
00:21:36,610 --> 00:21:39,820
100 tests a day, so you're going
to have constraints on that.

402
00:21:39,820 --> 00:21:42,750
The second is, politically, you
might have constraints, too.

403
00:21:42,750 --> 00:21:44,520
It might be tricky
for Greece, if they

404
00:21:44,520 --> 00:21:47,290
decide that they're not going
to let in anyone from Sweden.

405
00:21:47,290 --> 00:21:48,750
So there might be
different quotas,

406
00:21:48,750 --> 00:21:50,820
or there might be
other reasons to say,

407
00:21:50,820 --> 00:21:54,480
we have to think about
some broader types of risks

408
00:21:54,480 --> 00:21:56,340
and benefits in these cases.

409
00:21:56,340 --> 00:21:58,360
So that's also challenging.

410
00:21:58,360 --> 00:22:00,690
One way you can think about
implementing this is this

411
00:22:00,690 --> 00:22:04,680
could essentially change your
policy class that is reasonable.

412
00:22:04,680 --> 00:22:06,720
So instead of your
policy class saying

413
00:22:06,720 --> 00:22:09,190
you can make any decision
for any individual,

414
00:22:09,190 --> 00:22:12,180
you may now have a
population-level constraint

415
00:22:12,180 --> 00:22:13,680
as well.

416
00:22:13,680 --> 00:22:15,360
This is something
that my lab has

417
00:22:15,360 --> 00:22:18,810
thought about some with our
partner, Sharad Goel, who's

418
00:22:18,810 --> 00:22:20,670
at the Harvard Kennedy School.

419
00:22:20,670 --> 00:22:22,170
And there, we've
thought about cases

420
00:22:22,170 --> 00:22:24,420
where you might have resource
constraints and fairness

421
00:22:24,420 --> 00:22:27,120
constraints that mean that
you can't just make decisions

422
00:22:27,120 --> 00:22:28,480
for people individually.

423
00:22:28,480 --> 00:22:31,620
But you need to think
about overall trade-offs,

424
00:22:31,620 --> 00:22:33,930
in terms of your
policy quality, that

425
00:22:33,930 --> 00:22:35,850
happen at the population level.

426
00:22:35,850 --> 00:22:38,640
The reason that's important
is because it often introduces

427
00:22:38,640 --> 00:22:40,230
a lot of challenges
computationally,

428
00:22:40,230 --> 00:22:43,848
when you can't just think of
each individual separately.

429
00:22:43,848 --> 00:22:46,140
All right, so we won't be
able to cover all of the ways

430
00:22:46,140 --> 00:22:49,177
that they handle
this algorithmically.

431
00:22:49,177 --> 00:22:50,760
But I encourage you
to read the paper,

432
00:22:50,760 --> 00:22:52,480
if you're interested
in this space.

433
00:22:52,480 --> 00:22:54,300
And I think it's a
really beautiful example

434
00:22:54,300 --> 00:22:56,460
of using reinforcement
learning, particularly

435
00:22:56,460 --> 00:22:59,362
multi-armed bandits,
to tackle this problem.

436
00:22:59,362 --> 00:23:01,320
One of the things that
they had to do-- so this

437
00:23:01,320 --> 00:23:02,140
was a real system.

438
00:23:02,140 --> 00:23:03,400
They really deployed
it in Greece.

439
00:23:03,400 --> 00:23:04,900
I think, when I
talked to Hamsa, she

440
00:23:04,900 --> 00:23:06,790
said it came
together in a month.

441
00:23:06,790 --> 00:23:09,070
It was a really amazing effort.

442
00:23:09,070 --> 00:23:11,820
And then one of the interesting
things they also had to do here

443
00:23:11,820 --> 00:23:14,625
is to understand how much
of an impact it made.

444
00:23:14,625 --> 00:23:16,500
Because they weren't
going to do a randomized

445
00:23:16,500 --> 00:23:19,240
controlled trial in
COVID to understand this.

446
00:23:19,240 --> 00:23:22,830
So another interesting thing
that this paper looks at

447
00:23:22,830 --> 00:23:25,830
is using offline methods,
like the batch methods

448
00:23:25,830 --> 00:23:27,870
you've been seeing
in the past, to try

449
00:23:27,870 --> 00:23:31,608
to estimate the counterfactual
of how much impact this had.

450
00:23:31,608 --> 00:23:33,150
So I think it's a
really nice example

451
00:23:33,150 --> 00:23:34,860
of a lot of the different
ideas that we've

452
00:23:34,860 --> 00:23:35,943
been seeing in this class.

453
00:23:38,260 --> 00:23:38,780
All right.

454
00:23:38,780 --> 00:23:42,338
So that's one of the many, many
ways that bandits are useful.

455
00:23:42,338 --> 00:23:43,630
Clinical trials is another one.

456
00:23:43,630 --> 00:23:46,960
A/B testing, ad placement,
there's many, many others

457
00:23:46,960 --> 00:23:47,620
as well.

458
00:23:47,620 --> 00:23:50,078
But I think this is a really
nice example in public health.

459
00:23:50,078 --> 00:23:50,578
OK.

460
00:23:50,578 --> 00:23:51,500
So now let's continue.

461
00:23:51,500 --> 00:23:53,792
We're going to talk
about, specifically,

462
00:23:53,792 --> 00:23:56,000
some of the algorithms that
could be relevant to this

463
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:57,880
and, in particular,
Thompson sampling, which

464
00:23:57,880 --> 00:24:02,610
is particularly relevant to
this kind of batch setting.

465
00:24:02,610 --> 00:24:03,110
All right.

466
00:24:03,110 --> 00:24:05,150
I'm going to do, very
quickly, just notation.

467
00:24:05,150 --> 00:24:07,850
Remember, regret is the
opportunity loss per one step.

468
00:24:07,850 --> 00:24:10,070
Total regret is the
total opportunity loss.

469
00:24:10,070 --> 00:24:13,360
We're using Q to denote
the expected reward

470
00:24:13,360 --> 00:24:14,445
for a particular arm.

471
00:24:17,750 --> 00:24:19,990
I'm blanking on who
suggested this last time.

472
00:24:19,990 --> 00:24:20,490
Forgive me.

473
00:24:20,490 --> 00:24:23,190
But someone came up
to me and said, hey,

474
00:24:23,190 --> 00:24:25,900
couldn't we have used
just a smarter, optimistic

475
00:24:25,900 --> 00:24:27,640
initialization?

476
00:24:27,640 --> 00:24:32,090
Do we have to actually have
these upper confidence bounds?

477
00:24:32,090 --> 00:24:34,870
And I think that's a very
reasonable suggestion.

478
00:24:34,870 --> 00:24:36,980
And that was a great
follow-up to some

479
00:24:36,980 --> 00:24:39,980
of the stuff we're going
to talk about today.

480
00:24:39,980 --> 00:24:42,025
So one simple thing
you can imagine

481
00:24:42,025 --> 00:24:43,400
you could do,
instead of worrying

482
00:24:43,400 --> 00:24:45,108
about these upper
confidence bounds which

483
00:24:45,108 --> 00:24:48,030
you have to update all the
time, is you just optimize--

484
00:24:48,030 --> 00:24:51,620
you just initialize your
q hat to some high value.

485
00:24:51,620 --> 00:24:55,610
And then you just update
that estimate over time.

486
00:24:55,610 --> 00:24:58,100
And when you do
that, you know that,

487
00:24:58,100 --> 00:25:01,580
eventually, you're going to
converge to the right thing.

488
00:25:01,580 --> 00:25:04,850
Asymptotically, with the
law of large numbers is--

489
00:25:04,850 --> 00:25:07,100
you're not changing
that initialized value.

490
00:25:07,100 --> 00:25:09,420
That initialized value may
or may not have been right.

491
00:25:09,420 --> 00:25:12,740
It'll be an upper bound, and
it'll just converge to it.

492
00:25:12,740 --> 00:25:14,630
So this is an interesting
thing you can do.

493
00:25:17,940 --> 00:25:21,000
The challenge with that
is that, in general, you

494
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:23,130
don't know how high to make it.

495
00:25:23,130 --> 00:25:27,637
And so if you make
it really high--

496
00:25:27,637 --> 00:25:29,970
let me just be clear here,
what I mean by "really high."

497
00:25:29,970 --> 00:25:31,890
Often, this might
be much, much larger

498
00:25:31,890 --> 00:25:34,570
than the actual range
of possible rewards.

499
00:25:34,570 --> 00:25:37,150
So maybe your arm rewards
can be between 0 and 1.

500
00:25:37,150 --> 00:25:39,360
And you initialize this to 70.

501
00:25:39,360 --> 00:25:41,460
So sometimes the
initialization might

502
00:25:41,460 --> 00:25:45,180
be far higher than what
is actually practical.

503
00:25:45,180 --> 00:25:47,310
It does encourage a
lot of exploration

504
00:25:47,310 --> 00:25:49,620
early on, which might
be really valuable.

505
00:25:49,620 --> 00:25:52,830
But in general, unless you get
the value exactly right, which

506
00:25:52,830 --> 00:25:54,420
you generally can't know--

507
00:25:54,420 --> 00:25:58,200
because that's why you're trying
to learn, in the first place.

508
00:25:58,200 --> 00:26:00,880
Then you can still lock
on to a suboptimal action.

509
00:26:00,880 --> 00:26:03,360
And what do I mean by "lock
on," is that you converge

510
00:26:03,360 --> 00:26:06,970
to a suboptimal action and then
you never try anything again,

511
00:26:06,970 --> 00:26:10,260
which means you'd
get linear regret.

512
00:26:10,260 --> 00:26:12,840
The other thing
that's bad is that,

513
00:26:12,840 --> 00:26:18,690
if you initialize Q too high,
then you're also just not going

514
00:26:18,690 --> 00:26:19,900
to benefit from--

515
00:26:19,900 --> 00:26:21,900
you're going to be making
bad decisions for much

516
00:26:21,900 --> 00:26:23,420
longer than you
actually need to do.

517
00:26:25,960 --> 00:26:29,760
Even though, in theory, this
could be a good thing to do or--

518
00:26:29,760 --> 00:26:30,930
sorry.

519
00:26:30,930 --> 00:26:33,510
In principle, you might imagine,
this is a good thing to do.

520
00:26:33,510 --> 00:26:36,600
In reality, it's
very hard to set.

521
00:26:36,600 --> 00:26:41,310
Now, it's also an
interesting question

522
00:26:41,310 --> 00:26:46,050
of how you might do this
with function approximation.

523
00:26:46,050 --> 00:26:47,890
I know you didn't
implement deep Q-learning.

524
00:26:47,890 --> 00:26:49,950
But if you think back
to deep Q-learning,

525
00:26:49,950 --> 00:26:54,870
where we used a neural network
to represent the Q function,

526
00:26:54,870 --> 00:26:57,820
do you guys think it is
easy to initialize that,

527
00:26:57,820 --> 00:27:01,321
so the values are optimistic?

528
00:27:01,321 --> 00:27:01,890
Let's see.

529
00:27:01,890 --> 00:27:03,340
At least [MUTED]
shaking his head.

530
00:27:03,340 --> 00:27:04,768
Why not?

531
00:27:04,768 --> 00:27:05,310
You're right.

532
00:27:05,310 --> 00:27:06,852
It's just [INAUDIBLE]
for the network

533
00:27:06,852 --> 00:27:08,680
to output specific value.

534
00:27:08,680 --> 00:27:09,480
Yeah, it's hard.

535
00:27:09,480 --> 00:27:09,630
Right?

536
00:27:09,630 --> 00:27:11,200
Maybe you could train
it on fake data.

537
00:27:11,200 --> 00:27:13,075
But then you'd have to
know how big the Q is.

538
00:27:13,075 --> 00:27:16,900
Yeah, in general, this is
really-- if it was a table,

539
00:27:16,900 --> 00:27:18,900
it's at least easy
to write down, like,

540
00:27:18,900 --> 00:27:20,158
90 for all of those things.

541
00:27:20,158 --> 00:27:21,450
And that's what you initialize.

542
00:27:21,450 --> 00:27:24,270
In a deep neural network,
it's really unclear,

543
00:27:24,270 --> 00:27:26,430
how you initialize
those parameters,

544
00:27:26,430 --> 00:27:28,600
so that, for all the states
that you would reach,

545
00:27:28,600 --> 00:27:31,450
you would have even a good
shot of it being optimistic.

546
00:27:31,450 --> 00:27:33,810
So I think that's another
challenge here, is--

547
00:27:33,810 --> 00:27:36,780
and that's a challenge for a
lot of the optimism algorithms

548
00:27:36,780 --> 00:27:38,490
we'll see, in general,
is can we do it

549
00:27:38,490 --> 00:27:42,030
with function approximation.

550
00:27:42,030 --> 00:27:45,853
Now, there's a lot of work on
thinking about how to do things

551
00:27:45,853 --> 00:27:47,020
with function approximation.

552
00:27:47,020 --> 00:27:48,228
And we'll get into that soon.

553
00:27:51,222 --> 00:27:54,880
So if you do carefully choose
the initialization value,

554
00:27:54,880 --> 00:27:58,710
you can get good
performance under a new way

555
00:27:58,710 --> 00:28:01,380
of measuring what good
performance actually means.

556
00:28:01,380 --> 00:28:02,190
OK.

557
00:28:02,190 --> 00:28:04,910
So let's go back to regret.

558
00:28:04,910 --> 00:28:08,460
So in regret, we just
try to think about

559
00:28:08,460 --> 00:28:10,710
how do we quantify
the performance

560
00:28:10,710 --> 00:28:13,030
as we make lots of decisions.

561
00:28:13,030 --> 00:28:15,090
So T, here, is the number
of decisions we make.

562
00:28:15,090 --> 00:28:16,965
And we're just trying
to think, in this case,

563
00:28:16,965 --> 00:28:20,465
about how many decisions
we make over time.

564
00:28:20,465 --> 00:28:23,240
Let me see if the pen
is finally charged.

565
00:28:23,240 --> 00:28:25,490
Not today.

566
00:28:25,490 --> 00:28:27,020
So we could either
be making lots

567
00:28:27,020 --> 00:28:29,690
of little mistakes or
infrequent, large ones.

568
00:28:29,690 --> 00:28:36,770
And what you might
imagine that we want to do

569
00:28:36,770 --> 00:28:41,120
is to think about a
different form of loss.

570
00:28:41,120 --> 00:28:47,820
And so we're in, particular,
another form of performance.

571
00:28:47,820 --> 00:28:49,858
That is going to be PAC.

572
00:28:49,858 --> 00:28:51,400
So let's draw what
that'll look like.

573
00:28:54,530 --> 00:28:59,130
So I think I drew this last
time, but I'll draw it again.

574
00:28:59,130 --> 00:29:00,860
So make this times step t.

575
00:29:00,860 --> 00:29:07,010
And this is Q of at, Q of the
actual arm that you pulled.

576
00:29:07,010 --> 00:29:12,512
And this is q of a star,

577
00:29:12,512 --> 00:29:16,730
So let's imagine that you
have an algorithm that

578
00:29:16,730 --> 00:29:24,470
is pulling arms, like the
following, all right, which

579
00:29:24,470 --> 00:29:25,240
means that--

580
00:29:25,240 --> 00:29:27,240
then maybe sometimes it's
pulling the right arm,

581
00:29:27,240 --> 00:29:29,350
hopefully.

582
00:29:29,350 --> 00:29:31,790
So in this case,
sometimes the algorithm

583
00:29:31,790 --> 00:29:34,370
is doing something that's
just a little bit suboptimal,

584
00:29:34,370 --> 00:29:37,970
and sometimes it is making
a really big mistake.

585
00:29:37,970 --> 00:29:41,390
So what we can do here
is we can quantify

586
00:29:41,390 --> 00:29:43,580
how big our mistakes are.

587
00:29:43,580 --> 00:29:49,010
And you might have
a situation where

588
00:29:49,010 --> 00:29:53,290
you say optimal
performance is really hard.

589
00:29:53,290 --> 00:29:57,000
It's really hard to learn
what everyone's perfect ad

590
00:29:57,000 --> 00:29:58,780
preferences are or
things like that.

591
00:29:58,780 --> 00:30:00,810
Maybe I'm going to
relax my criteria.

592
00:30:00,810 --> 00:30:03,070
I'm not going to require
optimal performance,

593
00:30:03,070 --> 00:30:05,560
but I want pretty
good performance.

594
00:30:05,560 --> 00:30:08,100
I want epsilon optimal.

595
00:30:08,100 --> 00:30:15,420
So what we do, in this
case, is we count every time

596
00:30:15,420 --> 00:30:16,810
we make a bad decision.

597
00:30:16,810 --> 00:30:19,530
Meaning, something that is
worse than epsilon optimal.

598
00:30:19,530 --> 00:30:21,410
And otherwise, we
think of all of those

599
00:30:21,410 --> 00:30:23,660
as basically being in an
equivalence class of optimal.

600
00:30:26,460 --> 00:30:28,920
So that's going to
be what we think

601
00:30:28,920 --> 00:30:31,080
about when we think about PAC.

602
00:30:31,080 --> 00:30:31,580
OK.

603
00:30:31,580 --> 00:30:33,960
So I'll define what that is.

604
00:30:33,960 --> 00:30:36,143
So a PAC algorithm--
and raise your hands

605
00:30:36,143 --> 00:30:37,810
if you've seen this
in machine learning.

606
00:30:37,810 --> 00:30:39,185
If you've taken
machine learning,

607
00:30:39,185 --> 00:30:40,960
you might have seen PAC.

608
00:30:40,960 --> 00:30:41,460
OK.

609
00:30:41,460 --> 00:30:43,500
Yeah, so at least one
or two people have.

610
00:30:43,500 --> 00:30:45,333
So, often, in machine
learning, particularly

611
00:30:45,333 --> 00:30:48,510
if it's a machine learning
class that includes some theory,

612
00:30:48,510 --> 00:30:51,250
they'll talk about PAC and
probably approximately correct

613
00:30:51,250 --> 00:30:51,750
algorithms.

614
00:30:51,750 --> 00:30:53,333
And that's where
this idea comes from.

615
00:30:53,333 --> 00:30:55,920
So it came from the
machine learning community,

616
00:30:55,920 --> 00:30:57,900
and then reinforcement
learning borrowed it.

617
00:30:57,900 --> 00:31:02,190
So the idea in a PAC algorithm
is that on each time step,

618
00:31:02,190 --> 00:31:05,460
a PAC algorithm is going to
choose an action whose value is

619
00:31:05,460 --> 00:31:06,790
epsilon optimal.

620
00:31:06,790 --> 00:31:09,060
Meaning, the value of
the action that's taken

621
00:31:09,060 --> 00:31:12,940
is at least the value of the
optimal action minus epsilon.

622
00:31:12,940 --> 00:31:16,270
So that means that
we're in this region,

623
00:31:16,270 --> 00:31:19,930
with high probability on
all but a polynomial number

624
00:31:19,930 --> 00:31:22,200
of time steps.

625
00:31:22,200 --> 00:31:24,600
So essentially, it's
saying that the majority

626
00:31:24,600 --> 00:31:27,310
of the time, your algorithm
is making good decisions.

627
00:31:27,310 --> 00:31:29,830
Good, here, being defined
as epsilon optimal.

628
00:31:29,830 --> 00:31:31,783
But sometimes we'll
make bad decisions.

629
00:31:31,783 --> 00:31:33,700
But we're going to say,
with high probability,

630
00:31:33,700 --> 00:31:35,075
the total number
of bad decisions

631
00:31:35,075 --> 00:31:36,550
we make is not too many.

632
00:31:36,550 --> 00:31:38,730
What we mean by "not too
many" here is something

633
00:31:38,730 --> 00:31:41,530
that's polynomial, in
your problem parameters.

634
00:31:41,530 --> 00:31:44,680
So that generally means the
number of actions you have,

635
00:31:44,680 --> 00:31:48,130
epsilon, delta, et cetera.

636
00:31:48,130 --> 00:31:51,730
As you might expect,
if epsilon is smaller,

637
00:31:51,730 --> 00:31:54,730
generally, the number of
samples you need will go up.

638
00:31:54,730 --> 00:31:57,050
Normally, something like
1 over epsilon squared.

639
00:31:57,050 --> 00:32:00,590
So if you care about
being more optimal,

640
00:32:00,590 --> 00:32:02,583
you're going to need more data.

641
00:32:02,583 --> 00:32:04,000
Or, in other words,
your algorithm

642
00:32:04,000 --> 00:32:06,040
might make bad
decisions for longer.

643
00:32:06,040 --> 00:32:08,980
If delta is smaller,
meaning that you

644
00:32:08,980 --> 00:32:11,360
want this to hold with
higher probability,

645
00:32:11,360 --> 00:32:14,020
you'll also need more data.

646
00:32:14,020 --> 00:32:16,400
And if there are a lot of
actions to learn about,

647
00:32:16,400 --> 00:32:18,580
in general, you need more data.

648
00:32:18,580 --> 00:32:22,030
So it gives us some notion of
the complexity of the problem

649
00:32:22,030 --> 00:32:23,230
to learning.

650
00:32:23,230 --> 00:32:25,630
So this is a different type of--

651
00:32:25,630 --> 00:32:29,230
a lot of algorithms, you can
get both PAC guarantees for

652
00:32:29,230 --> 00:32:30,710
and regret guarantees.

653
00:32:30,710 --> 00:32:33,170
But it is just a different
notion of optimality.

654
00:32:36,092 --> 00:32:38,960
Most of the PAC algorithms
for reinforcement learning

655
00:32:38,960 --> 00:32:40,760
are based on either
optimism, like what

656
00:32:40,760 --> 00:32:43,340
we've seen from last lecture,
or Thompson sampling, which

657
00:32:43,340 --> 00:32:45,620
we're going to see
later in this lecture.

658
00:32:45,620 --> 00:32:49,790
And there do exist PAC
algorithms that just

659
00:32:49,790 --> 00:32:52,580
initialize everything
to a really high value.

660
00:32:52,580 --> 00:32:54,410
I don't know of any
practical algorithms

661
00:32:54,410 --> 00:32:56,990
that do that, ones that
people use in practice.

662
00:32:56,990 --> 00:32:59,850
But there is theory and
papers about that case,

663
00:32:59,850 --> 00:33:03,090
so it is possible to do.

664
00:33:03,090 --> 00:33:03,590
All right.

665
00:33:03,590 --> 00:33:06,453
And we'll see more
stuff about PAC shortly.

666
00:33:06,453 --> 00:33:07,620
Let me just give an example.

667
00:33:07,620 --> 00:33:09,770
So remember back
to our fake trying

668
00:33:09,770 --> 00:33:12,600
to learn how to
treat broken toes

669
00:33:12,600 --> 00:33:16,160
example from last time, where
we had surgery and taping, buddy

670
00:33:16,160 --> 00:33:18,050
taping the toes together.

671
00:33:18,050 --> 00:33:20,070
Again, this is not
medical advice.

672
00:33:20,070 --> 00:33:24,050
Imagine that this
is-- epsilon is 0.05.

673
00:33:24,050 --> 00:33:26,960
So in this case, before
we thought about this

674
00:33:26,960 --> 00:33:29,820
is what the optimal sequence
of actions you should take--

675
00:33:29,820 --> 00:33:31,778
but, of course, you don't
know that because you

676
00:33:31,778 --> 00:33:32,870
don't have data.

677
00:33:32,870 --> 00:33:36,090
If you had this sequence
of actions, [INAUDIBLE]

678
00:33:36,090 --> 00:33:39,000
and optimistic algorithm,
this would be the regret

679
00:33:39,000 --> 00:33:42,660
you would get in each case.

680
00:33:42,660 --> 00:33:45,480
But under the PAC case--

681
00:33:45,480 --> 00:33:48,080
let's see if I can
type this here.

682
00:33:48,080 --> 00:33:53,210
Under the PAC case,
this would be epsilon.

683
00:33:56,535 --> 00:33:58,470
So the important
thing to notice here

684
00:33:58,470 --> 00:34:01,140
is that, because
the reward of a2

685
00:34:01,140 --> 00:34:05,800
is within the epsilon bound of
a1, which is the optimal action,

686
00:34:05,800 --> 00:34:08,429
this action would also
be considered optimal.

687
00:34:08,429 --> 00:34:12,130
So from the perspective of
the PAC algorithm definition,

688
00:34:12,130 --> 00:34:14,850
this would be not
denoted as a mistake.

689
00:34:14,850 --> 00:34:19,469
The only mistakes would be
when the algorithm takes a3.

690
00:34:19,469 --> 00:34:21,840
So when we talk about
this PAC definition

691
00:34:21,840 --> 00:34:26,710
here of counting up the
number of time steps,

692
00:34:26,710 --> 00:34:28,810
we don't make a
really good decision.

693
00:34:28,810 --> 00:34:31,739
The only decisions that would
count for that, in this setting,

694
00:34:31,739 --> 00:34:34,199
is the a3 decisions.

695
00:34:34,199 --> 00:34:36,699
In contrast to that, when
we talk about regret,

696
00:34:36,699 --> 00:34:38,750
anything that's
suboptimal counts.

697
00:34:38,750 --> 00:34:42,470
So you get penalized for
all the a2 decisions.

698
00:34:42,470 --> 00:34:45,090
I thought we were
allowed to make mistakes

699
00:34:45,090 --> 00:34:49,230
for a polynomial
number of steps.

700
00:34:49,230 --> 00:34:51,400
Yes, you are allowed,
and it still will be PAC.

701
00:34:51,400 --> 00:34:52,480
That's exactly right.

702
00:34:52,480 --> 00:34:55,320
But I'm just pointing out here
that the-- so the only actions

703
00:34:55,320 --> 00:34:58,280
you're taking that count towards
that polynomial is a3 here.

704
00:34:58,280 --> 00:34:59,470
It's not a2.

705
00:34:59,470 --> 00:35:02,630
Whereas, a3 and a2 count
towards your regret.

706
00:35:02,630 --> 00:35:03,130
OK?

707
00:35:03,130 --> 00:35:03,630
Yeah,

708
00:35:03,630 --> 00:35:08,470
So does screening become easier
if I gradually reduce epsilon?

709
00:35:08,470 --> 00:35:09,230
Good question.

710
00:35:09,230 --> 00:35:14,080
So normally, in these cases,
you fix epsilon in advance.

711
00:35:14,080 --> 00:35:19,300
And it defines the
number of samples

712
00:35:19,300 --> 00:35:22,810
you're going to need for each of
the actions or states in actions

713
00:35:22,810 --> 00:35:23,990
in the MDP case.

714
00:35:23,990 --> 00:35:25,720
So it's like an
exploration term,

715
00:35:25,720 --> 00:35:27,910
and you keep track of counts.

716
00:35:27,910 --> 00:35:32,055
There are algorithms-- with
me and my former PhD student,

717
00:35:32,055 --> 00:35:33,430
[MUTED], part of
the work that we

718
00:35:33,430 --> 00:35:36,055
did there was to talk about what
if you want to have guarantees

719
00:35:36,055 --> 00:35:37,810
over many epsilons at once.

720
00:35:37,810 --> 00:35:39,310
I'm thinking more
along the lines

721
00:35:39,310 --> 00:35:44,200
of epsilon greedy algorithms
we did in [INAUDIBLE] where,

722
00:35:44,200 --> 00:35:47,290
because we gradually
reduce epsilon [INAUDIBLE]

723
00:35:47,290 --> 00:35:49,900
to the [INAUDIBLE].

724
00:35:49,900 --> 00:35:51,970
If that's the
same, they can be--

725
00:35:51,970 --> 00:35:53,028
do the same thing there.

726
00:35:53,028 --> 00:35:54,320
Yeah, it's a little bit subtle.

727
00:35:54,320 --> 00:35:55,070
It's a great question.

728
00:35:55,070 --> 00:35:56,800
So in general, the
bounds will depend,

729
00:35:56,800 --> 00:35:58,760
something like 1
over epsilon squared.

730
00:35:58,760 --> 00:36:00,453
So if your epsilon
is going to 0,

731
00:36:00,453 --> 00:36:02,620
that will say that you have
to do an infinite amount

732
00:36:02,620 --> 00:36:04,600
of exploration.

733
00:36:04,600 --> 00:36:06,160
If you're interested,
I have this.

734
00:36:06,160 --> 00:36:08,035
I mean, one of our papers
thinks about trying

735
00:36:08,035 --> 00:36:11,200
to have simultaneous bounds
over lots of epsilons.

736
00:36:11,200 --> 00:36:13,730
But in general, the
basic version of this,

737
00:36:13,730 --> 00:36:16,780
you commit to an
epsilon in advance.

738
00:36:16,780 --> 00:36:19,150
Great questions.

739
00:36:19,150 --> 00:36:19,700
All right.

740
00:36:19,700 --> 00:36:22,870
So going back to where we
are and reminding ourselves,

741
00:36:22,870 --> 00:36:24,320
in terms of algorithms--

742
00:36:24,320 --> 00:36:25,820
this relates to
your epsilon greedy.

743
00:36:25,820 --> 00:36:28,600
Constant e-greedy, decaying
e-greedy and optimistic

744
00:36:28,600 --> 00:36:30,940
initialization all have
the problem, in general,

745
00:36:30,940 --> 00:36:33,110
of having sublinear--

746
00:36:33,110 --> 00:36:35,180
of having bad performance.

747
00:36:35,180 --> 00:36:37,940
It's, in theory, possible
to have sublinear regret.

748
00:36:37,940 --> 00:36:41,410
But you often need to
have stronger knowledge

749
00:36:41,410 --> 00:36:42,670
than is known.

750
00:36:42,670 --> 00:36:45,657
Optimistic initialization also
can have the PAC guarantees

751
00:36:45,657 --> 00:36:46,740
that we just talked about.

752
00:36:51,190 --> 00:36:52,840
And I guess I'll just
say, too, you can

753
00:36:52,840 --> 00:36:55,780
convert these results into--

754
00:36:55,780 --> 00:36:58,810
so epsilon greedy is
not a PAC algorithm.

755
00:36:58,810 --> 00:37:01,270
But you can think
about different types

756
00:37:01,270 --> 00:37:04,540
of other exploration strategies
and whether or not they're PAC.

757
00:37:04,540 --> 00:37:07,820
And we'll get back
into those soon.

758
00:37:07,820 --> 00:37:08,320
OK.

759
00:37:08,320 --> 00:37:10,110
Let's jump into
Bayesian bandits.

760
00:37:10,110 --> 00:37:12,890
They're a pretty elegant idea.

761
00:37:12,890 --> 00:37:16,580
So, so far, we've made
almost no assumptions

762
00:37:16,580 --> 00:37:18,300
about our reward distribution.

763
00:37:18,300 --> 00:37:20,240
So we've maybe said
they're bounded.

764
00:37:20,240 --> 00:37:21,750
It can be between 0 and 1.

765
00:37:21,750 --> 00:37:24,135
And that's basically all
we needed for Hoeffding.

766
00:37:24,135 --> 00:37:25,260
We need them to be bounded.

767
00:37:25,260 --> 00:37:27,560
We needed them to be--

768
00:37:27,560 --> 00:37:31,070
they're independent and
identically distributed.

769
00:37:31,070 --> 00:37:33,420
But we haven't made
any other assumptions.

770
00:37:33,420 --> 00:37:34,940
So we haven't said
it's Gaussian,

771
00:37:34,940 --> 00:37:37,820
or it's a Bernoulli or
something else we might know.

772
00:37:37,820 --> 00:37:40,110
And when we're being
Bayesian about this,

773
00:37:40,110 --> 00:37:41,960
we're actually going
to leverage knowledge

774
00:37:41,960 --> 00:37:44,300
we have about the structure
of the way the rewards are

775
00:37:44,300 --> 00:37:45,068
generated.

776
00:37:45,068 --> 00:37:46,610
And what I mean by
that is, normally,

777
00:37:46,610 --> 00:37:50,510
some particular statistical
model, so it's a Gaussian model.

778
00:37:50,510 --> 00:37:53,570
Or it's a Bernoulli
model, things like that.

779
00:37:53,570 --> 00:37:55,520
And the reason that
that might be helpful

780
00:37:55,520 --> 00:37:57,020
is that, often, if
we're doing these

781
00:37:57,020 --> 00:37:59,255
in a domain like public
health or others,

782
00:37:59,255 --> 00:38:01,130
people might know lots
of information about--

783
00:38:01,130 --> 00:38:02,330
[SNEEZES]

784
00:38:02,330 --> 00:38:03,810
--what the reward structure is.

785
00:38:03,810 --> 00:38:04,580
Bless you.

786
00:38:04,580 --> 00:38:07,700
And could we leverage that to
get better algorithms and better

787
00:38:07,700 --> 00:38:09,360
performance?

788
00:38:09,360 --> 00:38:10,470
OK.

789
00:38:10,470 --> 00:38:13,050
So before we do
this, it's probably

790
00:38:13,050 --> 00:38:15,512
helpful to do just a quick
refresher on Bayesian inference.

791
00:38:15,512 --> 00:38:17,470
Some of you guys might
have done a lot of this.

792
00:38:17,470 --> 00:38:19,137
Some of you might
have done very little.

793
00:38:19,137 --> 00:38:24,921
We'll go through just a
quick reminder about this

794
00:38:24,921 --> 00:38:27,510
because this is going to
be used a lot for what

795
00:38:27,510 --> 00:38:29,340
we're going to see today.

796
00:38:29,340 --> 00:38:31,890
So the idea is that
we're going to start

797
00:38:31,890 --> 00:38:34,690
with a prior over the
unknown parameters.

798
00:38:34,690 --> 00:38:36,300
In our particular
case, that's going

799
00:38:36,300 --> 00:38:40,500
to be the unknown distribution
over the rewards for each arm.

800
00:38:40,500 --> 00:38:43,080
So it's like if we
have a coin flip.

801
00:38:43,080 --> 00:38:45,040
Or, if we think about
the toes example,

802
00:38:45,040 --> 00:38:47,310
what's the probability that
someone's going to heel,

803
00:38:47,310 --> 00:38:48,700
if they're given surgery?

804
00:38:48,700 --> 00:38:51,160
We don't know what that
parameter, theta is.

805
00:38:51,160 --> 00:38:53,280
And so we're going to
have a prior over what

806
00:38:53,280 --> 00:38:55,860
that theta could be.

807
00:38:55,860 --> 00:38:57,870
Once we're given
some observations

808
00:38:57,870 --> 00:39:00,010
about that parameter--
for example,

809
00:39:00,010 --> 00:39:01,770
if we observe, when
you do surgery,

810
00:39:01,770 --> 00:39:05,640
that someone was healed, that is
going to change your uncertainty

811
00:39:05,640 --> 00:39:07,180
over the unknown parameters.

812
00:39:12,147 --> 00:39:14,380
So let's do a
particular example.

813
00:39:14,380 --> 00:39:18,260
So if the reward of arm I is
the probability distribution

814
00:39:18,260 --> 00:39:20,630
depends on a
parameter of phi i, we

815
00:39:20,630 --> 00:39:22,970
have initial prior
over that parameter.

816
00:39:22,970 --> 00:39:25,590
Pull on arm, we
observe a reward.

817
00:39:25,590 --> 00:39:28,430
Then we can use Bayes'-- that
should be "Bayes'--" Bayes' rule

818
00:39:28,430 --> 00:39:29,125
to update that.

819
00:39:32,215 --> 00:39:34,370
And I think it's really
helpful to visualize

820
00:39:34,370 --> 00:39:37,250
how the priors change over time,
so we'll see that in an example

821
00:39:37,250 --> 00:39:40,970
shortly, just so you can see
what that might look like.

822
00:39:40,970 --> 00:39:47,500
So what we're going to
have here is Bayes' rule.

823
00:40:01,760 --> 00:40:02,490
All right.

824
00:40:02,490 --> 00:40:06,470
This is our prior probability
over the parameter governing

825
00:40:06,470 --> 00:40:09,830
the reward distribution
for this arm.

826
00:40:09,830 --> 00:40:12,090
This is the likelihood
of observing

827
00:40:12,090 --> 00:40:15,840
a particular reward given
a specific parameter value.

828
00:40:15,840 --> 00:40:19,530
And this is the probability of
seeing that reward in general.

829
00:40:19,530 --> 00:40:21,780
And when we do that,
this is Bayes' rule,

830
00:40:21,780 --> 00:40:24,600
and then we use it to update
what our new probability is

831
00:40:24,600 --> 00:40:27,420
over the parameter that
generates that reward.

832
00:40:27,420 --> 00:40:29,040
So in the case of
surgery, it would

833
00:40:29,040 --> 00:40:33,390
be before we had some
distribution over how successful

834
00:40:33,390 --> 00:40:37,330
we think surgery is on average,
we give surgery to someone,

835
00:40:37,330 --> 00:40:39,660
we update it, we observe
that they are healed.

836
00:40:39,660 --> 00:40:42,630
And then that changes what
we think about the underlying

837
00:40:42,630 --> 00:40:44,430
parameters.

838
00:40:44,430 --> 00:40:47,900
So, bring that out here.

839
00:40:50,660 --> 00:40:52,135
This is the prior probability.

840
00:40:52,135 --> 00:40:53,510
This is the
probability of reward

841
00:40:53,510 --> 00:40:55,330
given a particular parameter.

842
00:40:55,330 --> 00:40:57,870
This is the probability of
getting the reward in general.

843
00:40:57,870 --> 00:41:01,370
And we can rewrite this by
using the joint distribution

844
00:41:01,370 --> 00:41:06,440
of the reward and the parameter
and then marginalize out

845
00:41:06,440 --> 00:41:07,230
the parameter.

846
00:41:10,630 --> 00:41:11,130
All right.

847
00:41:14,680 --> 00:41:16,660
So this is beautiful.

848
00:41:16,660 --> 00:41:18,070
Oh, yeah?

849
00:41:18,070 --> 00:41:20,080
Can we go back to
the previous slide.

850
00:41:20,080 --> 00:41:22,270
I'm just kind of
confused on the setup.

851
00:41:22,270 --> 00:41:28,690
If I imagine that phi as a
primer for a Bernoulli variable

852
00:41:28,690 --> 00:41:31,030
and using background
knowledge, I

853
00:41:31,030 --> 00:41:33,410
have some prior as
to what it should be,

854
00:41:33,410 --> 00:41:36,520
what does it mean to have
a distribution over that?

855
00:41:36,520 --> 00:41:39,910
Yeah, it's a great--

856
00:41:39,910 --> 00:41:41,650
in general, it
may not be obvious

857
00:41:41,650 --> 00:41:42,770
that we can compute this.

858
00:41:42,770 --> 00:41:45,070
So for example, we're
going to see in some cases,

859
00:41:45,070 --> 00:41:46,130
this is analytic.

860
00:41:46,130 --> 00:41:48,680
You can analytically update
this, which is super elegant.

861
00:41:48,680 --> 00:41:53,200
What I mean in that
case is as a simple--

862
00:41:53,200 --> 00:41:56,510
so let's say-- we'll
see an example shortly.

863
00:41:56,510 --> 00:42:03,400
But phi i could be as the
probability recovery--

864
00:42:03,400 --> 00:42:04,550
I'll do this for surgery--

865
00:42:04,550 --> 00:42:06,390
for surgery.

866
00:42:06,390 --> 00:42:11,300
So this would be, say, 90% of
the time, someone's recovered.

867
00:42:11,300 --> 00:42:13,470
And let's say-- or
something like that.

868
00:42:13,470 --> 00:42:15,890
And this could be
a particular prior.

869
00:42:15,890 --> 00:42:20,090
So I could say, I
think my probability

870
00:42:20,090 --> 00:42:25,140
that your recovered mostly
from the surgery is 0.9.

871
00:42:25,140 --> 00:42:27,230
So I'm pretty confident
that the surgery

872
00:42:27,230 --> 00:42:29,420
is going to be highly
effective on average.

873
00:42:29,420 --> 00:42:36,500
But I think that there's some
probability that the surgery is

874
00:42:36,500 --> 00:42:38,180
not so effective.

875
00:42:38,180 --> 00:42:39,770
And then I would
say, well, I think

876
00:42:39,770 --> 00:42:41,580
that maybe it was
10% probability

877
00:42:41,580 --> 00:42:43,500
the surgery isn't as effective.

878
00:42:43,500 --> 00:42:47,600
But on average, people are
going to recover at rate 0.4

879
00:42:47,600 --> 00:42:49,415
with the surgery.

880
00:42:49,415 --> 00:42:51,290
And we'll see some
specific examples of this.

881
00:42:51,290 --> 00:42:53,055
This is not the priors
we're going to use,

882
00:42:53,055 --> 00:42:54,430
but this just
illustrates how you

883
00:42:54,430 --> 00:42:57,762
can have distributions over
distributions which can

884
00:42:57,762 --> 00:42:58,970
get confusing pretty quickly.

885
00:42:58,970 --> 00:43:01,540
But on the other hand, it's
also super elegant and a place

886
00:43:01,540 --> 00:43:04,000
where we can put
in prior knowledge,

887
00:43:04,000 --> 00:43:05,560
just like clinicians
and others may

888
00:43:05,560 --> 00:43:08,680
have information where they
can actually directly specify

889
00:43:08,680 --> 00:43:11,910
these priors.

890
00:43:11,910 --> 00:43:12,660
All right.

891
00:43:12,660 --> 00:43:15,880
And so there's many questions
you might have in this case of,

892
00:43:15,880 --> 00:43:17,690
like, where do these
priors come from?

893
00:43:17,690 --> 00:43:19,580
And even if we
have these priors,

894
00:43:19,580 --> 00:43:22,540
how do we do this calculation?

895
00:43:22,540 --> 00:43:27,050
So in general, this
is complicated.

896
00:43:27,050 --> 00:43:29,170
So you can see here,
you've got to have

897
00:43:29,170 --> 00:43:31,010
a functional form for this.

898
00:43:31,010 --> 00:43:33,170
This, in our case, was
like flipping a coin.

899
00:43:33,170 --> 00:43:37,480
And so if your coin has a bias
of 0.9, what's the probability

900
00:43:37,480 --> 00:43:38,570
you'd get reward 1?

901
00:43:38,570 --> 00:43:39,850
It would be 0.9.

902
00:43:39,850 --> 00:43:42,050
So you have to have a
probability distribution here,

903
00:43:42,050 --> 00:43:43,300
probability distribution here.

904
00:43:43,300 --> 00:43:45,080
You have to marginalize
one out over here.

905
00:43:45,080 --> 00:43:48,290
And when you do all of that,
you get your new posterior,

906
00:43:48,290 --> 00:43:51,540
which is after you
observe something, now,

907
00:43:51,540 --> 00:43:52,890
what is your new distribution?

908
00:43:52,890 --> 00:43:54,723
So you might imagine
that now I update this,

909
00:43:54,723 --> 00:43:56,690
maybe I see that the
surgery was successful.

910
00:43:56,690 --> 00:44:02,618
And I'm, like, oh, maybe I can
update this to be 0.95 and 0.05.

911
00:44:02,618 --> 00:44:04,250
So in general,
this is going to be

912
00:44:04,250 --> 00:44:07,490
computationally
tricky to do exactly

913
00:44:07,490 --> 00:44:09,480
without additional structure.

914
00:44:09,480 --> 00:44:11,190
There's lots of ways
to approximate it,

915
00:44:11,190 --> 00:44:13,650
but the really cool thing
is that in some cases,

916
00:44:13,650 --> 00:44:16,400
you can do this analytically.

917
00:44:16,400 --> 00:44:20,870
So this is idea of
these conjugate priors.

918
00:44:20,870 --> 00:44:24,600
So this is beautiful idea
of the exponential families.

919
00:44:24,600 --> 00:44:28,558
And if you have a
representation of your prior

920
00:44:28,558 --> 00:44:31,100
that is conjugate with-- this
is often called your likelihood

921
00:44:31,100 --> 00:44:32,660
function.

922
00:44:32,660 --> 00:44:35,660
Then, after you do
all of this updating,

923
00:44:35,660 --> 00:44:38,210
this new thing is in the
same statistical family

924
00:44:38,210 --> 00:44:41,060
as what this was in before.

925
00:44:41,060 --> 00:44:44,170
And we'll see some specific
examples of this in a second.

926
00:44:44,170 --> 00:44:47,090
So the high level, really
beautiful idea in this case

927
00:44:47,090 --> 00:44:48,570
is that it's analytic.

928
00:44:48,570 --> 00:44:51,740
When you do all of this,
let's say this was initially

929
00:44:51,740 --> 00:44:54,020
a Gaussian, this is still
going to be a Gaussian

930
00:44:54,020 --> 00:44:55,285
if you use conjugate priors.

931
00:44:58,604 --> 00:45:01,050
So let's see how to
do this for Bernoulli.

932
00:45:01,050 --> 00:45:03,285
So for Bernoulli, there
is a conjugate prior,

933
00:45:03,285 --> 00:45:04,160
which is really cool.

934
00:45:04,160 --> 00:45:07,067
And the conjugate prior is
called a beta distribution.

935
00:45:07,067 --> 00:45:09,650
And it's going to have a really
nice, beautiful interpretation

936
00:45:09,650 --> 00:45:11,130
that we'll see in just a second.

937
00:45:11,130 --> 00:45:13,100
So the equation looks terrible.

938
00:45:13,100 --> 00:45:15,050
The equation says
the probability

939
00:45:15,050 --> 00:45:16,490
of a particular theta--

940
00:45:16,490 --> 00:45:19,760
remember, this is the
bias of your coin--

941
00:45:19,760 --> 00:45:21,860
given some alpha and
beta-- these are just

942
00:45:21,860 --> 00:45:25,070
two other parameters-- is
theta to the alpha minus 1, 1

943
00:45:25,070 --> 00:45:27,260
minus theta of the
beta minus 1 times

944
00:45:27,260 --> 00:45:30,320
the gamma function of alpha
plus beta divided by gamma

945
00:45:30,320 --> 00:45:32,420
of alpha, gamma of beta.

946
00:45:32,420 --> 00:45:35,060
So this looks fairly
terrible, but it

947
00:45:35,060 --> 00:45:38,550
is conjugate, which means that
after we observe something,

948
00:45:38,550 --> 00:45:41,640
our new posterior is also
going to be the same.

949
00:45:41,640 --> 00:45:45,130
But it turns out that it has
a really simple explanation,

950
00:45:45,130 --> 00:45:48,000
a really simple
intuition, which is,

951
00:45:48,000 --> 00:45:52,200
imagine you start with your
prior being a beta alpha beta.

952
00:45:52,200 --> 00:45:54,390
And then you observe
a reward that's

953
00:45:54,390 --> 00:45:57,370
either 0 or 1 because your
variable is just 0 or 1.

954
00:45:57,370 --> 00:45:59,730
It's a Bernoulli.

955
00:45:59,730 --> 00:46:02,580
Then your new beta,
your posterior,

956
00:46:02,580 --> 00:46:09,860
is just r plus alpha,
comma, 1 minus r plus alpha.

957
00:46:09,860 --> 00:46:11,100
What does this mean?

958
00:46:11,100 --> 00:46:15,130
If you observed a 1, then you
increment your first parameter.

959
00:46:15,130 --> 00:46:17,780
It's like you increase
the number of successes.

960
00:46:17,780 --> 00:46:21,680
If you observe a 0, you increase
this number, the second number,

961
00:46:21,680 --> 00:46:23,930
like you increase the
number of failures.

962
00:46:23,930 --> 00:46:26,510
So you can think of what the
beta is doing is essentially

963
00:46:26,510 --> 00:46:30,570
just keeping track of how
many heads did you get,

964
00:46:30,570 --> 00:46:33,655
how many tails, or how many ones
did you get, and how many zeros?

965
00:46:33,655 --> 00:46:35,030
It's just keeping
track of those,

966
00:46:35,030 --> 00:46:37,820
and it can use those
to explicitly update

967
00:46:37,820 --> 00:46:40,970
what the probability
is of your theta.

968
00:46:40,970 --> 00:46:43,940
So it's really beautiful
because you don't--

969
00:46:43,940 --> 00:46:46,360
computationally, that's
really easy to keep track of.

970
00:46:46,360 --> 00:46:48,652
You're just going to add one
depending on what you see.

971
00:46:48,652 --> 00:46:50,810
And what you can think
of this here is being as,

972
00:46:50,810 --> 00:46:55,130
how confident are you in advance
of how many pseudocounts did you

973
00:46:55,130 --> 00:46:58,310
see of success versus failure?

974
00:46:58,310 --> 00:47:01,205
So, for example, if I'm really
confident that the surgery is

975
00:47:01,205 --> 00:47:03,080
going to be successful,
maybe I'm like, yeah,

976
00:47:03,080 --> 00:47:03,788
I'm so confident.

977
00:47:03,788 --> 00:47:07,580
It's as if I've seen 100
successful surgeries and only

978
00:47:07,580 --> 00:47:09,347
two failures.

979
00:47:09,347 --> 00:47:11,680
But if I'm really uncertain,
what I would do is I'd say,

980
00:47:11,680 --> 00:47:14,270
well, I'm going to treat like
one successful, one failure.

981
00:47:14,270 --> 00:47:15,480
I really don't know.

982
00:47:15,480 --> 00:47:17,525
And we'll see what this
looks like in just a sec.

983
00:47:21,930 --> 00:47:22,600
Excuse me.

984
00:47:22,600 --> 00:47:26,003
So now when we have
this, this is basically

985
00:47:26,003 --> 00:47:28,170
giving us a distribution
over the reward parameters,

986
00:47:28,170 --> 00:47:31,910
and we can use this to
actually make decisions.

987
00:47:31,910 --> 00:47:33,320
All right.

988
00:47:33,320 --> 00:47:36,090
So there's a couple of
different ways to do this.

989
00:47:36,090 --> 00:47:37,880
And one of the
ways to do this is

990
00:47:37,880 --> 00:47:40,100
by getting a confidence
interval, similar to what

991
00:47:40,100 --> 00:47:41,107
we've seen before.

992
00:47:41,107 --> 00:47:43,190
But the other thing is
called probability matching

993
00:47:43,190 --> 00:47:45,020
or Thompson sampling.

994
00:47:45,020 --> 00:47:47,755
And let's go through Thompson
sampling now and see an example.

995
00:47:51,770 --> 00:47:52,730
All right.

996
00:47:52,730 --> 00:47:57,250
So in probability
matching, we're

997
00:47:57,250 --> 00:47:59,590
going to assume we're in
the Bayesian bandit case.

998
00:47:59,590 --> 00:48:02,060
And what probability
matching does is says,

999
00:48:02,060 --> 00:48:03,670
OK, the way we might
want to explore

1000
00:48:03,670 --> 00:48:06,880
is by sampling actions according
to the probability that they're

1001
00:48:06,880 --> 00:48:10,000
optimal, given everything
I've seen so far.

1002
00:48:10,000 --> 00:48:13,510
So what it says is given
some history, which

1003
00:48:13,510 --> 00:48:15,190
is like the past
things I've tried

1004
00:48:15,190 --> 00:48:17,530
and whether I've gotten
ones or zeros for them,

1005
00:48:17,530 --> 00:48:21,310
I want to select a new action
based on the probability

1006
00:48:21,310 --> 00:48:24,400
that its true mean is
higher than the mean of all

1007
00:48:24,400 --> 00:48:25,050
the other arms.

1008
00:48:28,105 --> 00:48:29,480
And I'm not going
to tell you yet

1009
00:48:29,480 --> 00:48:31,948
that that's formally a good
thing to do in terms of regret,

1010
00:48:31,948 --> 00:48:34,240
but you might imagine, that's
a reasonable thing to do,

1011
00:48:34,240 --> 00:48:35,720
sort of says, oh,
well, if I think

1012
00:48:35,720 --> 00:48:38,630
that arm is likely to be
the optimal one with 60%

1013
00:48:38,630 --> 00:48:40,967
probability, I'll try
that with 60% probability.

1014
00:48:40,967 --> 00:48:42,800
And then if I think
there's another arm that

1015
00:48:42,800 --> 00:48:47,480
might be optimal, I'll try
that with 30% probability.

1016
00:48:47,480 --> 00:48:50,488
Now, in general, it's not clear
how you would compute this.

1017
00:48:50,488 --> 00:48:52,030
It seems kind of an
interesting idea.

1018
00:48:52,030 --> 00:48:53,570
It's not clear you
can compute it,

1019
00:48:53,570 --> 00:48:55,670
but it turns out there's
a really simple algorithm

1020
00:48:55,670 --> 00:48:58,400
to compute this.

1021
00:48:58,400 --> 00:49:00,660
So this is called
Thompson sampling.

1022
00:49:00,660 --> 00:49:03,170
And I think it was first
invented by Thompson

1023
00:49:03,170 --> 00:49:04,805
maybe in 1919.

1024
00:49:04,805 --> 00:49:07,570
Maybe 1919, maybe 1920.

1025
00:49:07,570 --> 00:49:10,790
Around 1919, Thompson sampling.

1026
00:49:10,790 --> 00:49:12,417
So it was around forever.

1027
00:49:12,417 --> 00:49:14,250
I mean, it's been around
for like 100 years.

1028
00:49:14,250 --> 00:49:16,920
But at least from the
machine learning perspective,

1029
00:49:16,920 --> 00:49:19,685
I think it was forgotten about
for the first, I don't know,

1030
00:49:19,685 --> 00:49:20,780
90 of those?

1031
00:49:20,780 --> 00:49:24,377
It really came back into
prominence about 2010, 2011,

1032
00:49:24,377 --> 00:49:26,210
when some people
discovered that it actually

1033
00:49:26,210 --> 00:49:30,310
had some really nice empirical
properties, unlike Hoeffding,

1034
00:49:30,310 --> 00:49:31,980
which has been used
for a long time.

1035
00:49:31,980 --> 00:49:33,380
How does Thompson sampling work?

1036
00:49:33,380 --> 00:49:35,670
We're going to have a
prior over each arm.

1037
00:49:35,670 --> 00:49:38,130
Then for each iteration,
what we're going to do

1038
00:49:38,130 --> 00:49:40,110
is we're going to sample
a reward distribution

1039
00:49:40,110 --> 00:49:41,160
from the posterior.

1040
00:49:41,160 --> 00:49:43,400
We'll see an example of
exactly what I mean by that.

1041
00:49:43,400 --> 00:49:47,170
We compute the action value
function, given that sample.

1042
00:49:47,170 --> 00:49:51,130
We take the arm that is
maximum, given those Q's.

1043
00:49:51,130 --> 00:49:53,528
We observe a reward, and
we update our posterior.

1044
00:49:53,528 --> 00:49:55,570
And then we're going to
do this many, many times.

1045
00:49:55,570 --> 00:49:57,570
And again, this will all
seem much more concrete

1046
00:49:57,570 --> 00:49:59,940
when we do an example.

1047
00:49:59,940 --> 00:50:02,700
And it's going to turn out
that this exactly implements

1048
00:50:02,700 --> 00:50:04,165
probability matching.

1049
00:50:04,165 --> 00:50:05,790
So let's come back
to this in a second,

1050
00:50:05,790 --> 00:50:08,110
and let's first do
a specific example,

1051
00:50:08,110 --> 00:50:11,160
because I think that will
make it a lot more concrete.

1052
00:50:11,160 --> 00:50:12,270
All right.

1053
00:50:12,270 --> 00:50:15,630
So let's go back to our
broken toes example.

1054
00:50:15,630 --> 00:50:18,180
What we're going to
do, so we're going

1055
00:50:18,180 --> 00:50:20,460
to place a prior over
each arms parameter,

1056
00:50:20,460 --> 00:50:24,000
and I'm going to choose beta 1,
1, What does a beta 1, 1 look

1057
00:50:24,000 --> 00:50:24,690
like?

1058
00:50:24,690 --> 00:50:26,777
That looks like the following.

1059
00:50:26,777 --> 00:50:28,360
I'm sorry, my pen
isn't working today,

1060
00:50:28,360 --> 00:50:29,440
otherwise that would
have been helpful.

1061
00:50:29,440 --> 00:50:30,755
But I'll draw it up here.

1062
00:50:40,230 --> 00:50:43,630
This is 0, this is
1, this is theta.

1063
00:50:43,630 --> 00:50:49,180
We know that for a Bernoulli
variable, the value for a theta

1064
00:50:49,180 --> 00:50:51,760
has to be somewhere
between 0 and 1

1065
00:50:51,760 --> 00:50:54,520
because you can either
always get 1 or always get

1066
00:50:54,520 --> 00:50:56,170
0 or somewhere in between.

1067
00:50:56,170 --> 00:50:58,510
What a beta 1, 1, looks
like-- so this is going

1068
00:50:58,510 --> 00:51:00,410
to be the probability of theta.

1069
00:51:00,410 --> 00:51:02,650
This is my prior.

1070
00:51:02,650 --> 00:51:06,490
What a beta 1, 1 looks
like is this, which

1071
00:51:06,490 --> 00:51:08,510
is a uniform distribution.

1072
00:51:08,510 --> 00:51:11,960
What it says is I have
no idea what theta is.

1073
00:51:11,960 --> 00:51:15,710
It could be 0, it could be 1, it
could be 0.5, it could be 0.7,

1074
00:51:15,710 --> 00:51:16,790
it could be 0.9.

1075
00:51:16,790 --> 00:51:19,040
It just says someone
is totally agnostic.

1076
00:51:19,040 --> 00:51:22,390
This is called often like an
uninformative prior, saying,

1077
00:51:22,390 --> 00:51:24,460
I have no idea
what my probability

1078
00:51:24,460 --> 00:51:26,540
is for surgery, et cetera.

1079
00:51:26,540 --> 00:51:28,095
But this is what
that looks like.

1080
00:51:31,534 --> 00:51:33,500
So this is our prior.

1081
00:51:33,500 --> 00:51:35,570
And now, what we're
going to do is

1082
00:51:35,570 --> 00:51:38,300
we're actually going to
sample a Bernoulli parameter,

1083
00:51:38,300 --> 00:51:41,910
given the prior of each
arm for the three arms.

1084
00:51:41,910 --> 00:51:43,820
So what does that mean?

1085
00:51:43,820 --> 00:51:48,740
That means I'm going to
sample something for surgery.

1086
00:51:48,740 --> 00:51:52,855
I'm going to sample
something for buddy taping,

1087
00:51:52,855 --> 00:51:54,230
and I'm going to
sample something

1088
00:51:54,230 --> 00:51:57,470
for nothing, for do nothing.

1089
00:51:57,470 --> 00:52:00,290
All of them have this
particular prior for now.

1090
00:52:00,290 --> 00:52:02,480
So for the first one,
it's like I'm just

1091
00:52:02,480 --> 00:52:05,240
sampling from a uniform
distribution between 0 and 1.

1092
00:52:05,240 --> 00:52:09,860
So it could be anything
between those 0 and 1.

1093
00:52:09,860 --> 00:52:11,840
Let me just check
which number I'm--

1094
00:52:11,840 --> 00:52:15,400
should I use the numbers I'm
going to use for the next ones?

1095
00:52:15,400 --> 00:52:21,590
So let's say, for example,
that I happen to sample 0.3.

1096
00:52:21,590 --> 00:52:23,840
That's a totally reasonable
thing that I could sample,

1097
00:52:23,840 --> 00:52:27,350
given this uniform
distribution between 0 and 1.

1098
00:52:27,350 --> 00:52:33,150
Then for buddy taping,
let's say I sample 0.5.

1099
00:52:33,150 --> 00:52:35,490
Again, a totally reasonable
thing I could sample,

1100
00:52:35,490 --> 00:52:37,110
given this distribution.

1101
00:52:37,110 --> 00:52:42,780
And for do nothing, I'm
going to sample 0.6.

1102
00:52:42,780 --> 00:52:45,210
So this is just
the distributions

1103
00:52:45,210 --> 00:52:48,720
that I have over my prior
over the parameters,

1104
00:52:48,720 --> 00:52:53,220
and this is a particular set
of parameters I could sample.

1105
00:52:53,220 --> 00:52:58,260
Given that, I now--

1106
00:52:58,260 --> 00:53:00,810
what Thompson sampling
says I should do

1107
00:53:00,810 --> 00:53:04,710
is I should select the
action that is maximal given

1108
00:53:04,710 --> 00:53:06,720
the parameters I've sampled.

1109
00:53:06,720 --> 00:53:09,000
So under these three
parameters, if you

1110
00:53:09,000 --> 00:53:11,130
want to maximize the
probability that someone

1111
00:53:11,130 --> 00:53:12,690
will recover from surgery--

1112
00:53:12,690 --> 00:53:14,790
or sorry, recover from their--

1113
00:53:14,790 --> 00:53:16,990
recover in terms of
their broken toe,

1114
00:53:16,990 --> 00:53:21,990
should I do surgery,
buddy taping, or nothing?

1115
00:53:21,990 --> 00:53:23,593
Which one has the
highest chance?

1116
00:53:23,593 --> 00:53:24,510
In this case, nothing.

1117
00:53:24,510 --> 00:53:27,242
In this case, nothing, right.

1118
00:53:27,242 --> 00:53:29,040
So as soon as you--
in our case, it's

1119
00:53:29,040 --> 00:53:30,670
pretty simple once
you see the theta,

1120
00:53:30,670 --> 00:53:33,390
because the theta is exactly
equal to the expected reward.

1121
00:53:33,390 --> 00:53:35,148
So what this would
say is in this case,

1122
00:53:35,148 --> 00:53:36,065
you should do nothing.

1123
00:53:39,450 --> 00:53:41,030
So this is going to be--

1124
00:53:53,270 --> 00:53:55,960
So this will say do nothing.

1125
00:53:55,960 --> 00:53:56,460
All right.

1126
00:53:56,460 --> 00:53:58,490
We're going to observe
the patient's outcome.

1127
00:53:58,490 --> 00:54:02,150
Now, in this case, we're going
to assume that doing nothing

1128
00:54:02,150 --> 00:54:03,600
is actually not so effective.

1129
00:54:03,600 --> 00:54:05,440
And so we're going
to observe a zero.

1130
00:54:05,440 --> 00:54:07,010
And now, what we're
going to do is

1131
00:54:07,010 --> 00:54:10,620
we're going to update the
posterior over doing nothing,

1132
00:54:10,620 --> 00:54:14,300
given that observation.

1133
00:54:14,300 --> 00:54:17,060
Now, the other two haven't--
the other two arms,

1134
00:54:17,060 --> 00:54:20,360
their prior hasn't changed
because we haven't gotten any

1135
00:54:20,360 --> 00:54:22,475
observations about
surgery or buddy taping.

1136
00:54:22,475 --> 00:54:24,350
The only thing we've
got an observation about

1137
00:54:24,350 --> 00:54:25,580
is doing nothing.

1138
00:54:25,580 --> 00:54:29,150
So what I said before--

1139
00:54:29,150 --> 00:54:30,180
so we have alpha.

1140
00:54:30,180 --> 00:54:32,100
This is our alpha
beta parameter.

1141
00:54:32,100 --> 00:54:33,570
So this is our prior.

1142
00:54:33,570 --> 00:54:37,400
In particular, it
was beta 1, 1 before.

1143
00:54:37,400 --> 00:54:40,880
And when I pull
this arm and I get

1144
00:54:40,880 --> 00:54:45,980
a reward of 0, what I said we
would do here is the first one

1145
00:54:45,980 --> 00:54:48,540
you can think of as being
the number of successes,

1146
00:54:48,540 --> 00:54:50,460
and the second one is
the number of failures.

1147
00:54:50,460 --> 00:54:58,161
So what this becomes is
it becomes beta 1, 2.

1148
00:54:58,161 --> 00:55:00,160
And that is going
to look different.

1149
00:55:05,590 --> 00:55:09,180
It's going to look like this.

1150
00:55:09,180 --> 00:55:14,880
This is a beta 1, 2.

1151
00:55:14,880 --> 00:55:16,235
And this is a beta 1, 1.

1152
00:55:21,544 --> 00:55:23,550
Does somebody want to
tell me intuitively

1153
00:55:23,550 --> 00:55:25,350
why it makes sense
that this looks

1154
00:55:25,350 --> 00:55:27,510
like this for doing nothing?

1155
00:55:27,510 --> 00:55:29,610
Does this put-- where
does this put weight

1156
00:55:29,610 --> 00:55:30,796
in terms of parameters?

1157
00:55:33,600 --> 00:55:38,540
Since we received a 0, we should
think it's more likely at 0.

1158
00:55:38,540 --> 00:55:41,170
Yeah, we should think of the
parameter value is likely lower.

1159
00:55:41,170 --> 00:55:43,230
And so we've shifted
our probability mass.

1160
00:55:43,230 --> 00:55:46,037
And so we're, like, OK, for
the things that we don't know,

1161
00:55:46,037 --> 00:55:48,120
we're still totally agnostic
about whether they're

1162
00:55:48,120 --> 00:55:49,140
effective or not.

1163
00:55:49,140 --> 00:55:52,180
For the thing that we just
tried, do nothing, we got a 0.

1164
00:55:52,180 --> 00:55:56,670
So it is more likely that
our actual theta is lower

1165
00:55:56,670 --> 00:56:00,750
because a lower theta in
general, will generate more 0's.

1166
00:56:00,750 --> 00:56:02,860
And so we've changed
our distribution.

1167
00:56:05,730 --> 00:56:08,520
Let's see what that
looks like here.

1168
00:56:08,520 --> 00:56:10,260
So this is our new posterior.

1169
00:56:10,260 --> 00:56:12,220
We're using again,
remember, this is conjugate.

1170
00:56:12,220 --> 00:56:15,810
So this is our new theta,
and we haven't changed it

1171
00:56:15,810 --> 00:56:17,400
for the other two.

1172
00:56:17,400 --> 00:56:20,250
Now, here's the next
important thing.

1173
00:56:20,250 --> 00:56:22,710
What we're going to
do now is we are--

1174
00:56:22,710 --> 00:56:26,220
so this is what that beta
looks like, just for beta 1, 2.

1175
00:56:26,220 --> 00:56:28,950
Now, we're going to do our
next step at Thompson sampling.

1176
00:56:28,950 --> 00:56:35,640
So what we have to do now
is we now have to resample.

1177
00:56:35,640 --> 00:56:40,510
So we are going to resample
where we have two distributions.

1178
00:56:40,510 --> 00:56:42,630
These two ones are a beta 1, 1.

1179
00:56:42,630 --> 00:56:45,990
And this one is a beta 1, 2.

1180
00:56:45,990 --> 00:56:48,840
We're going to throw away
all of our old parameters

1181
00:56:48,840 --> 00:56:50,070
from last time.

1182
00:56:50,070 --> 00:56:51,520
They were just samples.

1183
00:56:51,520 --> 00:56:54,150
We now have an updated
distribution for one

1184
00:56:54,150 --> 00:56:57,750
of the arms, and we have the
old one for the other two.

1185
00:56:57,750 --> 00:57:01,610
So in this case, we might
resample, and we would get this.

1186
00:57:05,010 --> 00:57:08,140
It's more likely now that
we would sample a theta

1187
00:57:08,140 --> 00:57:11,260
3, which is lower
because our beta puts

1188
00:57:11,260 --> 00:57:14,530
more weight on the lower part.

1189
00:57:14,530 --> 00:57:16,840
So this is what this
looks like here.

1190
00:57:16,840 --> 00:57:19,840
So under this, we're going to
pull arm one because it has

1191
00:57:19,840 --> 00:57:21,180
the highest expected success.

1192
00:57:24,450 --> 00:57:27,330
And that one is going
to give us a beta 2, 1,

1193
00:57:27,330 --> 00:57:30,990
because remember, again, that we
increment the first one in terms

1194
00:57:30,990 --> 00:57:33,700
of the number of successes
and number of failures.

1195
00:57:33,700 --> 00:57:36,680
So as you might expect, it's
exactly symmetric to this one.

1196
00:57:39,720 --> 00:57:42,420
And we have something
that looks like this.

1197
00:57:42,420 --> 00:57:45,520
I'm not being perfectly
precise of the intersection.

1198
00:57:55,330 --> 00:57:59,230
So now, again, we throw
away all the old parameters

1199
00:57:59,230 --> 00:58:00,360
that we've sampled so far.

1200
00:58:00,360 --> 00:58:03,498
So we're going to throw away
the 0.7, the 0.5, and the 0.3.

1201
00:58:03,498 --> 00:58:04,665
And we're going to resample.

1202
00:58:08,280 --> 00:58:13,200
So this time, let's imagine
we sample 0.71, 0.65, and 0.1.

1203
00:58:13,200 --> 00:58:15,720
And we again observe that
the outcome from surgery

1204
00:58:15,720 --> 00:58:16,780
is successful.

1205
00:58:16,780 --> 00:58:19,590
This is what a beta
3, 1 looks like.

1206
00:58:19,590 --> 00:58:21,610
So it stops looking
like a straight line.

1207
00:58:21,610 --> 00:58:23,730
It starts having a curve.

1208
00:58:23,730 --> 00:58:26,790
So I really like these graphs
because I feel like it gives one

1209
00:58:26,790 --> 00:58:30,480
a much better intuitive sense of
how as you get information that

1210
00:58:30,480 --> 00:58:34,770
translates to your
posterior over what

1211
00:58:34,770 --> 00:58:37,200
you think the theta likely is.

1212
00:58:37,200 --> 00:58:40,318
So as you see more
successes, it will

1213
00:58:40,318 --> 00:58:41,610
tend to go weighted to one way.

1214
00:58:41,610 --> 00:58:42,670
As you see more
failures, they're

1215
00:58:42,670 --> 00:58:43,753
weighted to the other way.

1216
00:58:43,753 --> 00:58:44,920
And as you might expect--

1217
00:58:44,920 --> 00:58:46,420
so we're not seeing
that right here.

1218
00:58:46,420 --> 00:58:50,640
But if you can
see cases where it

1219
00:58:50,640 --> 00:58:54,110
starts to concentrate in the
middle or somewhere in between,

1220
00:58:54,110 --> 00:58:58,040
just depends on what actual
observations you're getting.

1221
00:59:01,282 --> 00:59:03,610
So this is how Thompson
sampling works.

1222
00:59:03,610 --> 00:59:05,397
And I think-- so
let's say we did this,

1223
00:59:05,397 --> 00:59:07,730
then now we have this something
that's even more peaked.

1224
00:59:07,730 --> 00:59:11,410
We get another one, and you can
see it just continues to curve.

1225
00:59:11,410 --> 00:59:12,700
OK, yeah?

1226
00:59:12,700 --> 00:59:17,740
Could you use Thompson sampling
with random variables that just

1227
00:59:17,740 --> 00:59:21,670
have many more parameters
as opposed to just using

1228
00:59:21,670 --> 00:59:22,400
Bernoulli's?

1229
00:59:22,400 --> 00:59:23,810
Yes, absolutely.

1230
00:59:23,810 --> 00:59:24,310
Yeah.

1231
00:59:24,310 --> 00:59:25,850
And one of the examples
we'll see later today,

1232
00:59:25,850 --> 00:59:27,225
they're using it
for advertising,

1233
00:59:27,225 --> 00:59:30,090
and they have a large
number of features.

1234
00:59:30,090 --> 00:59:31,230
Yeah.

1235
00:59:31,230 --> 00:59:34,440
You can extend all of these to
the function approximation case.

1236
00:59:34,440 --> 00:59:36,300
Good question.

1237
00:59:36,300 --> 00:59:37,650
So I think one of the things--

1238
00:59:37,650 --> 00:59:39,667
I mean, obviously this
is a small example.

1239
00:59:39,667 --> 00:59:41,250
What we saw in this
particular example

1240
00:59:41,250 --> 00:59:45,000
I just did is that
we quickly started

1241
00:59:45,000 --> 00:59:48,870
to converge to a1 in this case.

1242
00:59:48,870 --> 00:59:51,750
Now, notice so far, we've
actually never pulled a2.

1243
00:59:51,750 --> 00:59:53,700
We had some probability
of pulling a2

1244
00:59:53,700 --> 00:59:58,140
because if we had sampled
a really high value for a2,

1245
00:59:58,140 --> 01:00:00,420
then we would have pulled it.

1246
01:00:00,420 --> 01:00:02,550
But we haven't done that yet.

1247
01:00:02,550 --> 01:00:07,050
And a1, which actually does
have generally, a higher

1248
01:00:07,050 --> 01:00:09,370
probability in this case
of having good outcomes,

1249
01:00:09,370 --> 01:00:11,160
is starting to be pulled.

1250
01:00:11,160 --> 01:00:13,650
So it's quite different
than the optimism methods

1251
01:00:13,650 --> 01:00:16,415
because in optimism, we had to
at least pull each arm once,

1252
01:00:16,415 --> 01:00:18,540
so we could even start to
initialize our confidence

1253
01:00:18,540 --> 01:00:19,170
bounds.

1254
01:00:19,170 --> 01:00:20,350
That's not the case here.

1255
01:00:20,350 --> 01:00:22,630
We already have a prior
over what the values are,

1256
01:00:22,630 --> 01:00:24,380
all of these, and we
can immediately start

1257
01:00:24,380 --> 01:00:25,630
using that to make decisions.

1258
01:00:29,960 --> 01:00:30,460
All right.

1259
01:00:30,460 --> 01:00:33,270
So what is Thompson
sampling doing

1260
01:00:33,270 --> 01:00:36,430
when we're doing these
polls, and what results

1261
01:00:36,430 --> 01:00:37,430
do we have in this case?

1262
01:00:40,930 --> 01:00:45,680
So what it is doing in this
case is it's-- well, actually,

1263
01:00:45,680 --> 01:00:49,013
let me just step back because
I wanted to get to the example.

1264
01:00:49,013 --> 01:00:50,930
So I went through that
part a little bit fast.

1265
01:00:50,930 --> 01:00:54,490
Let me just go to how
the matching is working.

1266
01:00:54,490 --> 01:00:56,680
So let's just go back
to here for a second.

1267
01:00:56,680 --> 01:01:00,100
So what we can see in this case
is what Thompson sampling is

1268
01:01:00,100 --> 01:01:02,740
actually doing, is
that each time point,

1269
01:01:02,740 --> 01:01:06,316
it is trying to select actions
according to this probability.

1270
01:01:08,890 --> 01:01:11,680
And it'll often end
up being optimistic

1271
01:01:11,680 --> 01:01:13,840
in the face of
uncertainty because we're

1272
01:01:13,840 --> 01:01:19,510
doing an argmax with respect
to our empirical estimates.

1273
01:01:19,510 --> 01:01:22,100
But it won't-- in general,
as you might imagine,

1274
01:01:22,100 --> 01:01:25,420
uncertain actions have a higher
probability of being the max.

1275
01:01:25,420 --> 01:01:29,965
So if you are really sure
that your parameter is at 0.5

1276
01:01:29,965 --> 01:01:32,590
and you have another parameter
you have very little information

1277
01:01:32,590 --> 01:01:34,810
about, you have a
beta 1, 1, then you're

1278
01:01:34,810 --> 01:01:37,780
more likely to accidentally
sample a much higher value

1279
01:01:37,780 --> 01:01:41,210
for that parameter.

1280
01:01:41,210 --> 01:01:47,178
So the elegant thing
here is that you can

1281
01:01:47,178 --> 01:01:48,470
think of this as the following.

1282
01:01:48,470 --> 01:01:50,930
This is really useful
also for the theory.

1283
01:01:50,930 --> 01:01:54,890
So in posterior matching,
that's this first line.

1284
01:01:54,890 --> 01:01:58,020
That's sampling things
according to this.

1285
01:01:58,020 --> 01:02:01,790
What Thompson sampling does is
it doesn't do that explicitly.

1286
01:02:01,790 --> 01:02:04,430
It just samples
a reward for each

1287
01:02:04,430 --> 01:02:07,560
of the-- like a reward parameter
for each of the different arms.

1288
01:02:07,560 --> 01:02:11,000
And then it picks the
one that's argmax.

1289
01:02:11,000 --> 01:02:13,280
And so it's really
elegant that that

1290
01:02:13,280 --> 01:02:16,390
is in fact, the same thing as
doing probability matching.

1291
01:02:19,560 --> 01:02:22,410
That gives us the
fact that that ends up

1292
01:02:22,410 --> 01:02:23,920
working in terms of these.

1293
01:02:23,920 --> 01:02:29,100
So the key idea in this case is
that as you're computing these

1294
01:02:29,100 --> 01:02:31,870
with respect to the data
that you have so far,

1295
01:02:31,870 --> 01:02:36,990
in fact, the probability that
Thompson sampling picks an arm

1296
01:02:36,990 --> 01:02:39,970
is exactly equal to
this true probability,

1297
01:02:39,970 --> 01:02:42,930
given all the data
you've seen so far.

1298
01:02:42,930 --> 01:02:43,787
I'll do a pointer.

1299
01:02:43,787 --> 01:02:45,370
If we have time at
the end, maybe I'll

1300
01:02:45,370 --> 01:02:49,260
to go through the proof briefly
for the Bayesian regret case.

1301
01:02:49,260 --> 01:02:51,900
But there's also a really nice
explanation of this inside

1302
01:02:51,900 --> 01:02:54,607
of Tor Lattimore and
Csaba Szepesv'ari's book.

1303
01:02:54,607 --> 01:02:55,690
There's quite a lot there.

1304
01:02:55,690 --> 01:02:58,140
This is a quite
mathematical version of it,

1305
01:02:58,140 --> 01:03:01,878
but it gives you some
really nice background.

1306
01:03:01,878 --> 01:03:02,795
Let's go back to here.

1307
01:03:08,250 --> 01:03:10,750
Let's first just talk about,
how do we evaluate performance?

1308
01:03:10,750 --> 01:03:12,800
So what we saw in
frequentist regret,

1309
01:03:12,800 --> 01:03:15,210
like what we saw last
time, is that we're

1310
01:03:15,210 --> 01:03:19,110
assuming a particular
unknown set of parameters.

1311
01:03:19,110 --> 01:03:22,570
Our arms are actually
0.9, 0.7, 0.6.

1312
01:03:22,570 --> 01:03:24,090
We just don't know
what they are.

1313
01:03:24,090 --> 01:03:27,720
And then our regret is
always evaluated with respect

1314
01:03:27,720 --> 01:03:32,610
to the optimal arm, given
that fixed set of parameters.

1315
01:03:32,610 --> 01:03:35,860
Bayesian regret assumes there's
this prior over the parameters.

1316
01:03:35,860 --> 01:03:37,390
And so when we
talk about regret,

1317
01:03:37,390 --> 01:03:39,630
we're actually taking an
expectation with respect

1318
01:03:39,630 --> 01:03:41,910
to that prior.

1319
01:03:41,910 --> 01:03:44,400
So it's still-- this looks
like exactly the same

1320
01:03:44,400 --> 01:03:46,050
as the frequentist
regret, but now we

1321
01:03:46,050 --> 01:03:48,155
have this outer
expectation over theta.

1322
01:03:52,470 --> 01:03:55,320
One of the key ideas
of this, in terms

1323
01:03:55,320 --> 01:03:57,300
of how one might prove
things in this case,

1324
01:03:57,300 --> 01:04:01,543
is if we think back to how we
proved some ideas around regret,

1325
01:04:01,543 --> 01:04:02,710
we didn't do the full proof.

1326
01:04:02,710 --> 01:04:04,330
I just try to give
some sketches.

1327
01:04:04,330 --> 01:04:07,770
One of the key ideas in the
proof for frequentist regret

1328
01:04:07,770 --> 01:04:10,140
in upper confidence
bounds is that we

1329
01:04:10,140 --> 01:04:13,800
try to construct these
upper confidence bounds UT

1330
01:04:13,800 --> 01:04:17,280
that we thought would be higher
than the true value of the arm

1331
01:04:17,280 --> 01:04:19,990
with high probability.

1332
01:04:19,990 --> 01:04:22,570
And we use that in order to
figure out how many times we

1333
01:04:22,570 --> 01:04:24,350
would pull suboptimal arms.

1334
01:04:24,350 --> 01:04:27,670
We leverage this fact.

1335
01:04:27,670 --> 01:04:30,610
So it turns out that you
can do Bayesian regret

1336
01:04:30,610 --> 01:04:33,350
bounds under a pretty
similar decomposition.

1337
01:04:33,350 --> 01:04:36,970
You can think about computing
an upper confidence bound

1338
01:04:36,970 --> 01:04:38,595
and the likelihood
that it'll hold.

1339
01:04:41,600 --> 01:04:43,350
We might come back
to that later today,

1340
01:04:43,350 --> 01:04:47,270
but I want to first get into
extending these up to higher

1341
01:04:47,270 --> 01:04:48,590
level settings as well.

1342
01:04:48,590 --> 01:04:50,630
Before we do that, I
just want to highlight

1343
01:04:50,630 --> 01:04:54,230
that if you try to
get standard bounds,

1344
01:04:54,230 --> 01:04:57,537
like what we saw last time, for
standard Thompson sampling--

1345
01:04:57,537 --> 01:04:59,870
and what I mean by that is
the type of Thompson sampling

1346
01:04:59,870 --> 01:05:01,460
I just showed you.

1347
01:05:01,460 --> 01:05:03,140
To my last check,
they don't actually

1348
01:05:03,140 --> 01:05:05,840
match the best bound for upper
confidence bound and frequentist

1349
01:05:05,840 --> 01:05:06,820
algorithms.

1350
01:05:06,820 --> 01:05:08,570
However, often
empirically, they can

1351
01:05:08,570 --> 01:05:11,328
be really effective algorithms.

1352
01:05:11,328 --> 01:05:13,370
And I'll just highlight
here that in general, you

1353
01:05:13,370 --> 01:05:16,220
can't compare directly
between Bayesian regret bounds

1354
01:05:16,220 --> 01:05:18,050
and frequentist
because one of them

1355
01:05:18,050 --> 01:05:22,745
is with respect to this
prior over parameters.

1356
01:05:22,745 --> 01:05:25,760
So let's look at that
for a particular domain

1357
01:05:25,760 --> 01:05:29,030
and why Thompson sampling
might be particularly helpful

1358
01:05:29,030 --> 01:05:30,920
for a lot of real world cases.

1359
01:05:30,920 --> 01:05:34,460
So this is a really nice paper
by Olivier Chapelle and Lihong

1360
01:05:34,460 --> 01:05:39,050
Li, which sort of re-initiated
a huge amount of interest

1361
01:05:39,050 --> 01:05:41,930
in Thompson sampling a
little over a decade ago.

1362
01:05:41,930 --> 01:05:44,130
So I think they were both
at Yahoo at the time,

1363
01:05:44,130 --> 01:05:45,710
if I remember, right?

1364
01:05:45,710 --> 01:05:48,030
They were thinking about
a contextual bandit case,

1365
01:05:48,030 --> 01:05:50,280
so they were thinking
about making news article

1366
01:05:50,280 --> 01:05:51,810
recommendations, et cetera.

1367
01:05:51,810 --> 01:05:54,320
And so there you
would have a context,

1368
01:05:54,320 --> 01:05:56,820
like you'd have a bunch of
features about an individual.

1369
01:05:56,820 --> 01:05:58,903
And also, often you would
have a bunch of features

1370
01:05:58,903 --> 01:06:01,700
about the arms, so explaining
maybe news articles,

1371
01:06:01,700 --> 01:06:05,973
and all the features, or
ads and stuff like that.

1372
01:06:05,973 --> 01:06:08,390
But we're still going to see
in the context of sampled iid

1373
01:06:08,390 --> 01:06:09,470
at each step.

1374
01:06:09,470 --> 01:06:13,800
So if I give a particular
ad at this time point,

1375
01:06:13,800 --> 01:06:16,290
it doesn't impact what's
going to happen to [MUTED]

1376
01:06:16,290 --> 01:06:17,920
So it's still a bandit.

1377
01:06:17,920 --> 01:06:20,640
There's no sequential
dependencies there.

1378
01:06:20,640 --> 01:06:21,700
Arms are articles.

1379
01:06:21,700 --> 01:06:24,310
Reward is binary.

1380
01:06:24,310 --> 01:06:27,090
Either you click
on it or you don't.

1381
01:06:27,090 --> 01:06:30,570
In this case, you can model
it using logistic regression

1382
01:06:30,570 --> 01:06:32,550
because you have
this binary output.

1383
01:06:32,550 --> 01:06:33,870
So what are we seeing here?

1384
01:06:33,870 --> 01:06:36,280
So this is CTR, which means
it's a clickthrough rate.

1385
01:06:36,280 --> 01:06:37,230
It's normalized
because they're not

1386
01:06:37,230 --> 01:06:39,647
going to tell us exactly what
they get on their real world

1387
01:06:39,647 --> 01:06:40,512
data.

1388
01:06:40,512 --> 01:06:41,970
The important thing
to look at here

1389
01:06:41,970 --> 01:06:44,370
is the x-axis, which is delay.

1390
01:06:44,370 --> 01:06:47,553
So in many cases, just like
what we saw for a public health

1391
01:06:47,553 --> 01:06:49,470
setting, there will be
some form of delay even

1392
01:06:49,470 --> 01:06:51,640
for online customer cases.

1393
01:06:51,640 --> 01:06:53,190
So Amazon will
show you something,

1394
01:06:53,190 --> 01:06:54,480
and they don't find
out for a little bit of

1395
01:06:54,480 --> 01:06:56,605
whether or not you're
clicking on it or whether you

1396
01:06:56,605 --> 01:06:57,630
bought the thing.

1397
01:06:57,630 --> 01:07:00,720
And so what they're comparing
their algorithms with here

1398
01:07:00,720 --> 01:07:01,780
is the following.

1399
01:07:01,780 --> 01:07:04,155
So TS is Thompson sampling.

1400
01:07:04,155 --> 01:07:06,840
OTS is optimistic
Thompson sampling.

1401
01:07:06,840 --> 01:07:09,720
You can try to add in a little
bit of optimism in these.

1402
01:07:09,720 --> 01:07:12,630
UCB is upper confidence bound.

1403
01:07:12,630 --> 01:07:14,250
EG I think, is epsilon greedy.

1404
01:07:14,250 --> 01:07:17,320
And exploit is you
just do whatever

1405
01:07:17,320 --> 01:07:18,970
the mean looks like so far.

1406
01:07:18,970 --> 01:07:21,490
These are all hyperparameters.

1407
01:07:21,490 --> 01:07:24,350
As often is the case, the
hyperparameters matter,

1408
01:07:24,350 --> 01:07:26,290
so it's useful to look at these.

1409
01:07:26,290 --> 01:07:27,790
I think the really
interesting thing

1410
01:07:27,790 --> 01:07:31,280
to look at in this case is
to look across the time.

1411
01:07:31,280 --> 01:07:36,520
So if you-- this is the
shortest delay, and this

1412
01:07:36,520 --> 01:07:37,820
is the longest delay.

1413
01:07:37,820 --> 01:07:40,640
And you can see for
the blue algorithm,

1414
01:07:40,640 --> 01:07:43,490
it varies very little in
terms of its performance,

1415
01:07:43,490 --> 01:07:45,670
even if things
are delayed a lot.

1416
01:07:45,670 --> 01:07:52,180
But if you look at, say,
UCB, its performance

1417
01:07:52,180 --> 01:07:57,410
tends to drop a lot in terms
of as you have longer delay.

1418
01:07:57,410 --> 01:07:59,290
And so that is
one of the reasons

1419
01:07:59,290 --> 01:08:00,790
why you might want
to do Thompson

1420
01:08:00,790 --> 01:08:04,030
sampling in these cases.

1421
01:08:04,030 --> 01:08:06,790
So let's think more
about that and do a check

1422
01:08:06,790 --> 01:08:07,915
our understanding.

1423
01:08:07,915 --> 01:08:10,540
So let's think about an online
news website with lots of people

1424
01:08:10,540 --> 01:08:11,785
logging in every second.

1425
01:08:11,785 --> 01:08:13,660
Often, someone will come
online before you've

1426
01:08:13,660 --> 01:08:15,995
seen the outcome of
the previous person.

1427
01:08:15,995 --> 01:08:17,620
It asks you to select
all of the things

1428
01:08:17,620 --> 01:08:20,307
that you think are true as we
think about Thompson sampling

1429
01:08:20,307 --> 01:08:21,599
versus upper confidence bounds.

1430
01:09:40,490 --> 01:09:40,990
All right.

1431
01:09:40,990 --> 01:09:43,157
Why don't you compare your
answer to someone nearby?

1432
01:10:50,070 --> 01:10:51,830
So let's come back together.

1433
01:10:51,830 --> 01:10:56,610
So as we were just discussing,
we pointed out that Thompson

1434
01:10:56,610 --> 01:11:00,020
sampling could cause much worse
performance-- this one is true--

1435
01:11:00,020 --> 01:11:03,550
than optimism if the
prior is very misleading.

1436
01:11:03,550 --> 01:11:04,405
So this is true.

1437
01:11:23,470 --> 01:11:27,605
Because if for example, maybe
surgery is really effective

1438
01:11:27,605 --> 01:11:29,980
and someone starts off and
thinks surgery isn't effective

1439
01:11:29,980 --> 01:11:32,840
at all, and so you put a
lot of probability mass,

1440
01:11:32,840 --> 01:11:35,620
you could have a really
sharp prior on it over here,

1441
01:11:35,620 --> 01:11:37,450
then it could take a
long time essentially

1442
01:11:37,450 --> 01:11:39,790
for your data to
overwhelm your prior.

1443
01:11:39,790 --> 01:11:42,220
So this one can be a problem.

1444
01:11:42,220 --> 01:11:44,620
The first one is also true.

1445
01:11:44,620 --> 01:11:47,620
So if you think back
to the algorithms

1446
01:11:47,620 --> 01:11:50,470
that we saw last
time for optimism,

1447
01:11:50,470 --> 01:11:54,050
there is no randomness in
there, unless you have a tie.

1448
01:11:54,050 --> 01:11:56,892
So if your upper confidence
bound for arm one

1449
01:11:56,892 --> 01:11:58,600
is higher than the
upper confidence bound

1450
01:11:58,600 --> 01:12:00,310
for arm two and
arm three, you're

1451
01:12:00,310 --> 01:12:02,050
just going to take arm one.

1452
01:12:02,050 --> 01:12:04,970
And that's fine, but
if you have a delay,

1453
01:12:04,970 --> 01:12:07,400
that means you can't update
those upper confidence bounds.

1454
01:12:07,400 --> 01:12:09,490
So if the next customer
comes, you're, like, oh.

1455
01:12:09,490 --> 01:12:11,140
Or the next patient comes,
and you're, like, I still

1456
01:12:11,140 --> 01:12:12,110
think surgery is best.

1457
01:12:12,110 --> 01:12:13,360
I still think surgery is best.

1458
01:12:13,360 --> 01:12:15,700
And you're not going to
try anything different.

1459
01:12:15,700 --> 01:12:20,630
Whereas Thompson sampling just
has this prior or posterior.

1460
01:12:20,630 --> 01:12:23,690
And so if I have
someone come, I can just

1461
01:12:23,690 --> 01:12:26,480
sample from all of my priors.

1462
01:12:26,480 --> 01:12:28,380
And then if another
person comes,

1463
01:12:28,380 --> 01:12:30,300
I'll again sample
from my priors.

1464
01:12:30,300 --> 01:12:33,140
And so because of
this distribution

1465
01:12:33,140 --> 01:12:35,330
over parameters,
unless it's collapsed

1466
01:12:35,330 --> 01:12:36,903
to a delta function,
in which case

1467
01:12:36,903 --> 01:12:38,820
you know what the right
thing is to do anyway,

1468
01:12:38,820 --> 01:12:40,907
you'll get natural exploration.

1469
01:12:40,907 --> 01:12:43,490
So that's one of the really big
benefits of Thompson sampling,

1470
01:12:43,490 --> 01:12:45,450
is that even if you
don't get new data,

1471
01:12:45,450 --> 01:12:48,380
you naturally will try
out different things,

1472
01:12:48,380 --> 01:12:50,420
and that can be really helpful.

1473
01:12:50,420 --> 01:12:52,510
It is true that optimism
algorithms generally

1474
01:12:52,510 --> 01:12:54,260
are better than Thompson
sampling in terms

1475
01:12:54,260 --> 01:12:55,470
of their regret bounds.

1476
01:12:55,470 --> 01:12:58,460
That may or may not translate
to empirical benefits.

1477
01:12:58,460 --> 01:13:00,260
But they don't
actually necessarily

1478
01:13:00,260 --> 01:13:02,610
have strong regret
bounds for this setting.

1479
01:13:02,610 --> 01:13:04,020
So this is false.

1480
01:13:04,020 --> 01:13:05,660
And that's because
all the bounds

1481
01:13:05,660 --> 01:13:08,240
we've been talking
about so far, don't

1482
01:13:08,240 --> 01:13:10,058
think about that batch setting.

1483
01:13:10,058 --> 01:13:12,600
They're being derived for the
case where you get information,

1484
01:13:12,600 --> 01:13:16,940
you update your confidence
bounds, you continue.

1485
01:13:16,940 --> 01:13:20,390
So this highlights some
of the particular benefits

1486
01:13:20,390 --> 01:13:22,920
and the potential weaknesses
of Thompson sampling.

1487
01:13:22,920 --> 01:13:26,600
If your prior is reasonable and
you've got this delay or batch

1488
01:13:26,600 --> 01:13:28,940
setting, it can be very helpful.

1489
01:13:28,940 --> 01:13:32,630
If your prior is really
bad, it can take a long time

1490
01:13:32,630 --> 01:13:33,715
to get past that.

1491
01:13:37,960 --> 01:13:42,610
So before we end today, I
think an interesting question

1492
01:13:42,610 --> 01:13:46,630
to consider is whether or not
Thompson sampling is optimal.

1493
01:13:46,630 --> 01:13:48,752
Now, we can get nice regret
bounds for this case.

1494
01:13:48,752 --> 01:13:51,460
I know I didn't have a chance to
go through that particular proof

1495
01:13:51,460 --> 01:13:55,280
today, but it's not
optimal in general.

1496
01:13:55,280 --> 01:13:58,180
So it would be really cool
if we could get something

1497
01:13:58,180 --> 01:14:00,100
that was basically perfect.

1498
01:14:00,100 --> 01:14:02,230
You might imagine that
if you have a prior

1499
01:14:02,230 --> 01:14:04,780
and you have a known horizon
that you could compute

1500
01:14:04,780 --> 01:14:07,570
a decision policy that would
maximize your expected rewards,

1501
01:14:07,570 --> 01:14:09,850
given that prior
and the horizon.

1502
01:14:09,850 --> 01:14:11,660
So I haven't, at
least in this class,

1503
01:14:11,660 --> 01:14:13,760
taught you all the tools
you need to do that.

1504
01:14:13,760 --> 01:14:15,510
But at a high level,
you could think of it

1505
01:14:15,510 --> 01:14:18,890
as a Markov decision
process over parameters,

1506
01:14:18,890 --> 01:14:19,980
which is kind of wild.

1507
01:14:19,980 --> 01:14:22,820
So if any of you guys have taken
Mykel Kochenderfer's class--

1508
01:14:22,820 --> 01:14:25,360
actually, who's taken
Mykel's class, anybody here?

1509
01:14:25,360 --> 01:14:27,130
So you can think
of like a POMDP.

1510
01:14:27,130 --> 01:14:30,790
Your state is your parameters,
your actions are pulling things,

1511
01:14:30,790 --> 01:14:33,490
and then your belief state
is your new probability

1512
01:14:33,490 --> 01:14:34,750
of your parameters.

1513
01:14:34,750 --> 01:14:36,460
So it's really elegant.

1514
01:14:36,460 --> 01:14:38,990
In theory, you can
compute something

1515
01:14:38,990 --> 01:14:41,630
that will exactly maximize
your expected reward

1516
01:14:41,630 --> 01:14:43,247
by doing POMDP planning.

1517
01:14:43,247 --> 01:14:45,830
The problem, also for those of
you who've taken Mykel's class,

1518
01:14:45,830 --> 01:14:48,530
is that often, POMDP planning
is really intractable.

1519
01:14:48,530 --> 01:14:50,210
So it's often not
clear that we could

1520
01:14:50,210 --> 01:14:53,750
do this in a computationally
reasonable way.

1521
01:14:53,750 --> 01:14:55,730
In general, one
of the challenges

1522
01:14:55,730 --> 01:14:58,467
here is that if you
wanted to do this,

1523
01:14:58,467 --> 01:15:00,050
it would have a
decision policy that's

1524
01:15:00,050 --> 01:15:02,300
a function of the
history, which means

1525
01:15:02,300 --> 01:15:04,070
all the prior
actions you've taken

1526
01:15:04,070 --> 01:15:05,890
and all of the rewards
you've observed.

1527
01:15:05,890 --> 01:15:07,640
And that's going to
increase exponentially

1528
01:15:07,640 --> 01:15:09,223
with the number of
decisions you made.

1529
01:15:13,010 --> 01:15:15,870
So there's this idea
of an index policy.

1530
01:15:15,870 --> 01:15:17,420
And an index policy
says we don't

1531
01:15:17,420 --> 01:15:22,190
want to have to think about this
exponential history or state.

1532
01:15:22,190 --> 01:15:24,590
An index policy is, one,
a decision policy that

1533
01:15:24,590 --> 01:15:26,820
computes a real valued
index for each arm,

1534
01:15:26,820 --> 01:15:28,760
and it plays the arm
with the highest index,

1535
01:15:28,760 --> 01:15:32,420
using statistics only from
that arm and the horizon.

1536
01:15:32,420 --> 01:15:34,310
So that means I don't
have to pay attention

1537
01:15:34,310 --> 01:15:37,230
to this combinatorial
exponential thing.

1538
01:15:37,230 --> 01:15:40,380
I can just say for
this particular arm,

1539
01:15:40,380 --> 01:15:43,500
maybe what were my rewards
that I've observed so far,

1540
01:15:43,500 --> 01:15:46,200
and then I can use that
information to make decisions.

1541
01:15:46,200 --> 01:15:48,050
So, for example,
a greedy algorithm

1542
01:15:48,050 --> 01:15:51,140
which just relies on
your empirical average

1543
01:15:51,140 --> 01:15:54,200
of the performance for each
arm, is an index policy.

1544
01:15:54,200 --> 01:15:55,992
So it's an upper
confidence bound algorithm

1545
01:15:55,992 --> 01:15:58,158
because it just relies on
the upper confidence bound

1546
01:15:58,158 --> 01:16:00,060
for the rewards you've
seen for each arm.

1547
01:16:00,060 --> 01:16:02,930
So there are a lot
of index policies.

1548
01:16:02,930 --> 01:16:07,710
Surprisingly, there is an
index policy that is optimal.

1549
01:16:07,710 --> 01:16:11,490
So Gittins proved that there
exists an optimal policy

1550
01:16:11,490 --> 01:16:14,340
for maximizing the expected
discounted reward in a Bayesian

1551
01:16:14,340 --> 01:16:17,130
multi-armed bandit,
that you can compute,

1552
01:16:17,130 --> 01:16:18,720
that only depends
on these statistics

1553
01:16:18,720 --> 01:16:20,580
separately for each arms.

1554
01:16:20,580 --> 01:16:21,700
So that's really cool.

1555
01:16:21,700 --> 01:16:24,120
It means that it is
possible in some settings

1556
01:16:24,120 --> 01:16:27,960
to actually exactly optimize
your expected sum of discounted

1557
01:16:27,960 --> 01:16:30,485
rewards for these type
of Bayesian bandits.

1558
01:16:33,390 --> 01:16:35,530
Thompson sampling will
not do this in general.

1559
01:16:35,530 --> 01:16:37,080
So Thompson sampling
is generally not

1560
01:16:37,080 --> 01:16:39,360
equal to what the
Gittins index would be,

1561
01:16:39,360 --> 01:16:42,630
but it can still be a
very good thing to do.

1562
01:16:42,630 --> 01:16:43,180
All right.

1563
01:16:43,180 --> 01:16:45,270
So just to summarize
some of the things that

1564
01:16:45,270 --> 01:16:48,072
are useful to understand from
this part of the section.

1565
01:16:48,072 --> 01:16:49,530
And next time,
we're going to start

1566
01:16:49,530 --> 01:16:53,340
talking about these ideas for
sequential decision processes,

1567
01:16:53,340 --> 01:16:54,847
like Markov decision process.

1568
01:16:54,847 --> 01:16:56,680
You should be able to
define regret and PAC.

1569
01:16:56,680 --> 01:16:59,940
You should be able to prove
or know why the UCB bandit

1570
01:16:59,940 --> 01:17:02,580
algorithm has sublinear regret,
like up to the proof sketch

1571
01:17:02,580 --> 01:17:03,930
we did in class.

1572
01:17:03,930 --> 01:17:06,180
You should be able to give
an example of why e-greedy,

1573
01:17:06,180 --> 01:17:10,735
and greedy and pessimism
can result in linear regret.

1574
01:17:10,735 --> 01:17:12,360
I don't think you
need to be able to do

1575
01:17:12,360 --> 01:17:15,840
this for Gaussian rewards.

1576
01:17:15,840 --> 01:17:19,320
But you should be able to do
Thompson's sampling for the case

1577
01:17:19,320 --> 01:17:22,510
that we've just talked about,
at least in pseudocode land.

1578
01:17:22,510 --> 01:17:24,520
So if someone said, you
observe another count,

1579
01:17:24,520 --> 01:17:26,730
what would your
beta parameter be?

1580
01:17:26,730 --> 01:17:29,220
And then also that you should
be able to understand the UCB

1581
01:17:29,220 --> 01:17:31,650
bandit algorithm as
we've covered in class.

1582
01:17:31,650 --> 01:17:34,560
So we've been building
up all of these things

1583
01:17:34,560 --> 01:17:37,290
to think about now how we can do
exploration and data efficient

1584
01:17:37,290 --> 01:17:39,282
learning for
sequential processes.

1585
01:17:39,282 --> 01:17:40,740
So next time, we'll
think about how

1586
01:17:40,740 --> 01:17:43,013
to do this in a standard
decision process

1587
01:17:43,013 --> 01:17:44,430
as well as thinking
about, what do

1588
01:17:44,430 --> 01:17:46,470
we do when we're in really
large state spaces or really

1589
01:17:46,470 --> 01:17:48,595
large action spaces, and
how do we lift this all up

1590
01:17:48,595 --> 01:17:49,920
for function approximation?

1591
01:17:49,920 --> 01:17:52,100
I'll see you on Wednesday.

